Higgs boson in detail
Last summer, a message that scientists may have managed to detect the Higgs boson caused a real stir - and not without reason.
It is believed that this boson explains the mass of all the particles in the universe, nothing less.
In addition, this is the last particle whose exexistence has been predicted as part of the Standard Model - our best, or, at any rate "the least imperfect", description of the nature and the behavior of matter - but which could not have been detected in an experimental way earlier.
But physicists do not yet fully believe that we are talking about the Higgs boson.
We know, without the shadow of a doubt that we are dealing with a really new particle and that it strongly reminiscent of the Higgs boson predicted by the Standard Model.
In addition, the new data presented this week at a major physical congress in Kyoto seems to confirm this, although some data is still lacking for full confidence.
Let's imagine that we are talking about Higgs boson, since the odds seem to make a mistake seem minimal, and let's see what it means.
There is, in this world one law is completely unavoidable, which says that two things can not find themselves in the same place at the same time.
There is no way around this rule, don’t even try too diligently, otherwise hurt yourself.
But although the physics of elementary particles is a fairly strange world, it turns out that there is also a law of this nature in it: Pauli's principle of emptiness, which calculates that two particles cannot occupy the same space at the same point of the time, if they are in the same "quantum state" - this "state" consists, in general terms, in some of their characteristics.
On this basis, physicists divide the particles into two categories.
In one corner, we find respectable citizens called fermions who prudently obey Pauli’s principle.
In the other frustrated boson, a nesty gang of anarchists, who have no respect for nothing - at least for this principle, which means that they can quietly find themselves in the same place at the same time.
Berkeley’s Particle Adventure, these bosons are, in turn, are divided into two groups: Mesons that we will not talk about here, and the “capacity of the force”, through which the basic forces of nature are spreading and to in some way the Higgs boson belongs.
These very boson, it should be pointed out, not all such exotic creatures as you might think.
In fact, in fact, if you manage to read this chronicle, it’s thanks to an incredibly banal boson: a photon, or a “particle of light”, which is a “courier” of the electromagnetic force.
Indebtically, when a single particle with an electrical charge is accelorating or changing direction, this “violates” the electromial field in this particular location, approximately like a stone thrown into the pond.
From this “violation”, an electromagnetic Wave (severe or infrared radiation or ultraviolet radiation, etc.) is born, and this wave is nothing more than a photon - and, therefore, one of the bolognes of "strength carriers".
A Styrobging Fellow
The same thing happens with the Higgs bosoon, with the only difference that in order for this boson to appear, it must be “violated” another field, Higgs field.
However, Higgs field is much, much more stable than the electromagnetic field; to excruise it, it is necessary to reach a very, very high energies, as if it was about a frozen pond, the surface of which could only share a very large stone.
That's why a huge particle accelerator is needed, like the one that is operated in CERN - the Large Hadron Collider, which's a ring with a diameter of 27 km! - to achieve such huge energies.
An analogy with an Electromagnetic field is also useful to explain the relationship between the Higgs boson and the mass.
In fact, not all particles and not all materials interact with an electromagnetic field.
Some, such as magnets, interact, while others don’t: a piece of paper, for example, won’t stay on the freak.
In the same way, not all particles interact with the Higgs field: those that interact have a mass, while others (as a photon, by the way) don’t.
So, then, what "can bring" all these studies, asks Ms. Plamondon?
In scientific science, they are used to verify the correctness of the Standard Modal (CM), and this also allows physicists to find a discrepancy between the results of observations and CM forecasts.
In addition, some of them crave passionately crave such divergence, since the smallest inconsistencies can open the door for “new physics” and plug some of the Model’s holes.
The Latter, must be said, still has significant flaws, offering no explanation for gravity (oh!) or for dark matter, which forms about 80% of the universe.
But so there are no such discrepancies in CERN yet.
Consequences
Benefiance from these studies is more difficult to predict ancestinate daily life, but it would be a mistake to assume that it does not exist.
See: in the very beginning of the 60-ies the pioneers of laser research in Bella's laboratories could not even guess what kind of revolution their work will cause.
They foreshadowed their appendices in science, but nothing more.
Indebtly physicist Willard Boyle, who worked in Bell’s Laboratories, where a laser was invented in 1960 and who himself developed the first continuous laser (premises were impulsive) in 1962, said that the laser was originally seen rather as a “laboratory gadget”.
Subscribe...
And in addition, the application can be found for the tools that accompanies the study.
For example, the same Williard Boyle during his optical studies in 1969 developed a small light detector.
This sensor, although it did not at all its original purpose, now serves as an “eye” for all digital cameras in the world and has enjoinscended the Nobel Prize in physics in 2009.
This, of course, doesn’t mean that the work of the Large Hadron Collijøder will definitely change our lives, but this only shows, in fact, that everything can happen.
