To pay and sell without the help of the banks - with Bitcoin
A complete anti-alantty of the current monetary exchange, based on central banks, numbered transactions between the receiving parties with commission fees for their processing.
In addition, as often happens with these technologies, an obviously political vision: the belief that the current currency system, consisting of banking monopolies, leads to financial crises.
In fact, Bitcoin invented by Satoshi Nakamoto (pseudonym), is at the same time a virtual currency (but convertible into dollars, the euro ...) and a secure exchange protocol by BitTorrent, which allows you to exchange files between members of the same level.
Approximately 200 000 transactions have already been registered with 15 000 computers in the network.
Thousands of sites accept Bitcoin for donations or as a means of payment.
The rate of bitcoin, reaching a maximum of $ 30 (23 euros) in June 2011, two months later fell to $ 2 before returning today to about ten dollars (the rates are indicated on the website bitcoincharts.com).
There is nothing special in comparison with the global exchanging of real money or financial products.
Nevertheless, the European Central Bank (ECB) in its report on the virtual currency, published in October, became interested in this system.
He describes bitcoin as “the “best successful virtual currency”, “competing with the dollar or the euro” and “similar to ordinary currencies”.
Bitkoin differs from other types of virtual currencies like “loans” used to promote in a video game that are earned during a game or that can be bought (and sometimes exchanging).
The social network Facebook has also developed such a system.
But exchange operations are always overseeing and processes any central authority.
In Bitkoyn, all nodes of the network are simultaneously holders of the ledger, auditors, currency issuers, buyers and sellers.
How does this network work?
Each transaction between the two users actually happens between two e-mail addresses like an e-mail.
Only for each new payment, the user can choose a different address, thus providing an anonymity in this way.
A set of information related to this transaction is subscribed electronically, using a dual-cloud encryption system.
The network can also verify the autinacy of the transaction.
Thanks to the content of the file, it is possible to check that the bitcoins used in the Transaction really exist in a public account book sent all over the network.
The key stage is to register a new transaction in this book.
It passes through the solution of a mathematical problem sent to the computers, which, like a Temporary Central Bank, receives a privilege to add this new line.
We are talking about the file hashing phase, that is, the transformation of a large file into a digital signature, a short and unique.
The computers "taking" a new transaction and add a number to it, before "hashing" all together.
The goal is to find the number that will give a unique numerical signature (many nullies at the beginning).
As soon as this number is found, other nodes easily check that it is right.
The transaction in this way turns out to be inextricably associated with the chain of all other transactions; any modification will change a digital signature.
For the purpose of deceiving, the user very quickly (in less than a ten-mute minute) would wish to pay twice as much money, only one of these two transactions would be confirmed by the network - and the other would remain unconfirmed, since they have different electronic signatures.
The computer, which solved the problem, receives 50 bitcoins.
To avoid inflorescation, the award regularly shares in half, probably until the end of 2012.
The number of bitcoins in circulation is therefore limited to 21 million, but they are divided up to one hundred-mundnium, which leaves a reserve.
Difficulty of the problem also increases with an increase in computing power.
The life of the network has already experienced ups and falls.
Sites providing Bitcoin services have been attacked, and the bitcoins were stolen from reserves.
“Uselligent defect doesn’t concern the protocol itself,” assures Pier Nuaza, who has recently launched Paymium, a company that makes payments with real money using the Bitcoin network.
THE ECB also reported the possibility of terrifying the money languish through this anonymous service.
Cycling also lacks this drawback.
Large players such as Wikipedia refuse to accept donations of this kind.
Others, such as a WordPress blogging platform, allow them.
Recently, Adi Shamir and Dorith Rohn of the Weizmann Institute in Israel have analysed a counting book and found that almost 80% of bitcoins aren't in circulation.
Giant Sellies were announced in November.
"Thirty-thous of dollars have been exchanged," Jone Kholmqvist rejoiced at Coinabul, which converts bitcoin into gold.
Pier Nuaza, another author of this currency manual, firmly believes the potential of this technology as an operating network.
Its system, Paytunia, is the equivalentiary credit card equivalent (in real money) or a contactless payment via a mobile phone, but it uses Bitcoin to verify transactions that it is cheaper in this way.
Winner is better oversecting his or her personal data and may thus be anonymized.
The system is easy to implement for entrepreneurs who do not need to install new devices or software.
It's just enough to provide an address that you can "phot and recognize" with the phone, says Pier Nuase, assuring that it already has thousands of users.
There is a general tendency to retrieve hierarchical systems in favor of more terrestrial.
“Before it will be confirmed, it will take some time, but 2013 can be a turning point,” he predicts.
The ECB in its report assumes, by the way, to revise a variety of risks, which are now considered to be increased, in the case of the success of this system.
