Difficulties of the Egyptian Constitution
“This is President Mohamed Morsi’s new pricose step.
While Egypt is divided more than ever about the constitutional declaration, which temporarily gives it extraordinary powers, he decided to play wa-bank.
Chasing everyone by surprise, he announced Wednesday that the Constituent Constituent Assembly will vote on the final text the next day.
Exactly a week ago, the head of state provided the Assembly for two additional months to complete the work.
Soon it is already for two years since Egypt relies on the preliminary text, in which several amendments have been made, which weakens institutional stability and leads to legal inconsistencies.
This new initiative only strengthened the division of the country.
According to his opponents, the President persists in his “autocracial delusion”, continuing to “violate this word” and “trudle the law”.
In the camp of his adherents believe that this is a way to put an end to the institutional and political crisis, accelerating the transition process.
Referendum must be carried out within fifteen days.
A very short period of time, which forces the Brothers to renounce the intention to explain to the Ignampers the text, the article.
For the president, it is also a way to restore social and democratic legitimacy while dissatisfaction rages throughout the country.
Mohammed Morsi seems convinced that the Egyptians will vote positively, as he stated in an interview with the American weekly Taim.
Moreover, the appeal for an accelerated vote resembles an ultimatum to the Egyptian people: “Either you vote for my text, or I confiscate extraordinary powers”, which, it is assumed, should be completed after the adoption of the Constitution.
In this bizarre atmosphere, 85 members of the Constituent Assemblection, mostly Islamists, voted yesterday.
Most Liberals didn’t respond to challenges.
In mid-November, shortly before the constitutional declaration they squeezed the door, feeling not able to convey their opinion.
Representatives of human rights defenders, religious minorities or civil society have done the same.
In order for the necessary quorum to be reached, 11 people, reserve participants, were hastily added yesterday morning.
Some are very close to the Muslim Brotherhood.
Undoubtedly, in most cases the articles were voted unanimously.
By the way, the commentators amused the fact that one of the few disagreements of the day arose around ... the time for prayer, since some members of the committee considered that the hours of the Constituent Assembly were mismanted.
The text on which there was still a Vote yesterday, consists of 234 articles.
The object of universal attention, article 2 eventually remained identical to an identical article from the Constitution of the year of the year of 1971, which states that “chariat-principles are a fundamental source of law”.
Salafist parties, for which the implementation of Islamic law is one of the main requirements, hoped to replace the “principles” with “rules”, which would make it possible to more strictly apply.
For Islamists, the fact that this article has not been changed is a testament to their goodwill and respect for other members of Egyptian society.
"Lycemeria", - the Libertarians answer, who see in this only cerepod.
Because, in their opinion, the Islamization of the Constitution takes place through other articles.
They pointed, in Particular, article 220, which grants Al-Azhar University an advisable role, in particular with regard to scrutinizing the conformity of sharia laws.
According to Egypda specialist Sophy Pomier, this is alarming, since “people designed to make decisions have not been chosen and do not have any democratic legitimacy.”
“We can observe the ancestralism.”
Many Liberals, in addition, stole the fact that the next rector of this university seems to be much less temperate than the present.
“At the moment there is no specific religious intercession.
With this constitution, we remain within the framework of civil law.
Most of the lawyers who have been working on this text are not ulema, but people with a university education, some have studied in the French system,” sums up Alexis Bloe, who prepares his dissertation on the Egyptian constitutional transition.
But he admits that “acrifuges may arise around article 220, since the terms used in it are borrowed from religious vocabulary.
This applies, in particular, to "fiqh" [Islam jurisprudence].
And in the future there may be a question of whether the civilian judges are competent in order to make a decision on it.”
In addition to its religious aspect, the text, adopted yesterday, has been criticized, since it grants broad powers to the President of the Republic.
The Muslim Brotherhood claims that they have decreased significantly compared to what they were under the old regime.
Another disclusive moment: the powers of the army.
In accordance with the wishes of the military, the consideration of the defense budget will take place not in the Parliament, but in the National Defense Council.
It would also also not be prohibited for the trial of civilians in military courts, despite the requests for human rights associations.
the latter also expressed concern about the text, which they dedicated to freedom.
Responsibilities remain responsible for blasphemy, and anxiety is prohibited from now on, which can have serious consequences for freedom of expression, and especially for the freedom of the press.
In addition, none of the articles more talks about the protection of a woman, emphasizes Heba Moraef, of Hueman Rights Watch.
According to her, the only positive implication is the prohibition of torture in article 36.
A word that didn't appear in the previous Constitution.
While the Egyptian president was due to speak on television last night, the demonstrations are planned this afternoon.
Proponents of the head of state, in turn, will take to the streets on Saturday.
