And the Monastery of the Holy Cross in Jerusalem was erected on the place where, according to the Christian legend, a tree grew, from which a cross was made to crucifify Jesus.
In Jerusalem there is the most hilarious place for Judah - the Wreeting wall, which was left of the temple, destroyed by the Romans in 70 A.D.
According to tradition, people of different confessions leave here notes with desires, which are then fulfilled.
Making A Journey by Vertical
The ruins of Massada's fortress remained of a secret shelter from the enemies that Herod the Great built in 25 BC for his own family.
They are located on steep cleavages in the mountains at an altitude of 450 m above the ocean level.
And only those who are fond of alpinism can reach their own.
Left on this historical vertex delivers a lift on the rope road.
And in the north of the country at an altitude of 1600-2040 m is the famous ski resort of Hermon, on which a huge influx of tourists in the winter months is a huge inflogue.
To it from the foot of the mountain brings a shuttle station bus.
The total length of alpine ski slopes is 45 km.
According to an ancient legend, pagan gods supposedly lived on the top of the mountain.
To visit unique museums
There are about 300 constituenseecular museums in this country.
You won’t go to every single trip.
But at least five of them, the most interesting, it is worth visiting.
Among them - the Israel Museum, located not far from Knesset (Parliament).
It contains the most ancient Kumran Manuscripts, or Dead Sea scrolls found in the caves of the Judah desert and about 500 thousand archaeological and anthropological exhibits.
It's worth visiting the Museum of Art in Tel Aviv.
Its exhibrium is widely presented with canvases of Impressionists and Expressionists, Pissarro, Renoir, Cézanne, Sisley, Matisse, Modigliani, Chagall, Picasso.
You can visit Accra at Al-Bahá, which consists of several halls of ancient Turkish wa hinges with dummies of visitors and banners at that time.
In Caysaria, you should go to a unique private Rally Muséry, where you can admire Dali and Rodin’s sculptations.
There are no guides and souvenir shops.
Entrance is free and donations are categorically not accepted.
The fifth - the Holocaust Museum or Yad Vashem in Tel Aviv - speaks of one of the most dramatic pages of history.
The most terrifying section - the children's memorial - is built in memory of 1.5 million children destroyed in concentration camps and gas chambers.
You come into it - and you find yourself in absolute darkness.
The stars are shimmering.
And the names of the Jewish children and the names of the countries in which they died are heard.
“Equity is also mentioned there.
Healing
Israel's three main resort areas are located on the shores of the Mediterranean, the Red and Dead Sea.
On each of them there are swimming pools, aqua parks, dolphinaries and aquariums.
It is interesting that in the Red Sea they swim even in the winter months, since the water temperature there does not fall below 21 degrees Celsius, and the air is heated to 23 degrees.
The permafrost of the sea is even warmer, and they are bathed there all year round.
By the way, this the most extraordinary sea in the world is at the lowest point of the planet - 417 m below sea level.
Cozied water in it - a brine that easily keeps floating awkward even not at all able to float at all.
Landscapes are unreliably beautiful.
They come here to take a course of treatment using salt water - rapa, and healing the health of dermatitis, allergies, asthma, ecremas, arthritis, bronchitis, diabetes mellitus to put in order the fascinated nerves.
Touch the Mysteries of Antiquity
They are preserved by an ancient part of Tel Aviv - the town of Jaffa on the shores of the Mediterranean.
Through it passed the famous Maritime Path, connecting Egypt, Syria, Anatolia and Mesopotamia.
The city is mentioned in ancient Greek and ancient Egyptian legends.
According to legend, it was here that Noah built his ark, and Perseus saved the beauthist Andromeda, with whom they lived a long and happy life.
Tourists are very fond of wandering the narrow streets, named in honor of zodiac signs.
They say that if you touch the walls on the street of "his" sign, Fortune will become benevolent.
In Jaffa you can meet the newlyweds who come from all over Israel and even other countries at a photo session.
And in Caysaria - the city of Tsar Herod - you can wander through the Roman theater, "taking" the Crusaders' fortress.
In the period of the Roman period, Keisaria was the main city of Judah and the residence of Roman procurators, including Pontius Pilate.
A carefully refurgized theater is now used for evening concerts and opera performances.
The Tourist for a Note
Going to Israel, do not bother about your imperfect English: in the country about 30% of the population knows Russian.
It's better to take money on a trip not in euros, but in dollars, which are easily exchanged for shekels (now $ 1 - 3.8 shekels).
Urban transport - mainly buses, but in Jerusalem there is a high-speed tram, and in Haifa - the only metro line in the country, consisting consisting of six stations and connecting the Lower city with the Upper.
In fact, it is an underground funicular.
Driving in all types of public transport costs 6 shekels, for which you can drive with transfers for 1.5 hours.
In Israel, according to Judici’s tradition, the Shabha is celebrated on Saturday.
From the evening of Friday to sunset on Saturday stop working markets, shops, public transport.
And on Sunday in the morning the working week begins.
In many cafés, restaurants and hotels - only kosher meals, excluding pork, seafood, fish without scales and meals, in which milk is combined with meat.
They offer a large selection of meals from lamb and beef, soups and desserts prepared on coconut's milk, a traditional Jewish hummus paste, a variety of sauces, thrombothed chundy balls - thalales, fruits and vegetables.
There are no wretched dogs on the streets of Israeli cities.
But there are a lot of furnished cats, which camouflage "walking on their own."
In the evening they you can be seen sleeping even on the roofs of parked cars.
Murlyks love crowded places and don’t give up food.
