In the U.S. blossom workers of fast food and supermarkets
Through the work at the McDonalds check-in passes up to a quarter of all American teenagers
In recent few days, there has been a wave of protests protesting against low-wage at Walmart department stores and restaurants of popular fast-food chains such as McDonalds, Bourger Kying, Taco Bell, Wendis and Kentucky Freake Chicken.
While no one undertakes to predict whether this wave will turn into a ninth shaft or it is destined to excrete in the early stages.
The actions are supported by trade unions and a number of left-wing public organizations.
In addition to the increase in the modest salaries received by ordinary Walmart employees and fast-feeding systems, the purpose of the organizers of the protests is to create trade union organizations in them.
While this sector of the economy is almost not engulfed by a trade union movement.
46 cents a year?
The action began last week, after Thanksgiving in the so-called “Dark Friday”, when an orgy of cheap sell-offs fell on America, which captured millions of ordinary people and was sometimes accompanied by scuffles.
On this day, some Walmart employees, employing 2.2 million people around the world, left their workplaces and picketed, together with trade unions and left-wing activists, the corporation's stores whose merchandise is based on people of humble and medium-sized.
Walmart trades everything in the world, from diapers, hunting rifles and car batteries to vacuum cleaners, eggs and milk.
Products in its stores on an average of 8% to 27% are cheaper than in large supermarkets.
Therefore, many low-walking Walmart employees are only eaten at their jobs.
Availability and assortment of goods made Walmart one of the largest corporations in America.
According to Critics, Walmart can afford to sell the goods in part, because he pays little to its Employees.
The latter also complain of difficult working conditions, for example, the lack of forklifts or manual scanners.
The participants of the shares in “Black Friday” demanded to increase the salary and lamented the fact that the cost of medical insurance provided by the corporation jumped from $ 30 to $ 100 per month.
Typical Walmart worker who receives $ 9.5 an hour, this expense is not affordable, they said they said.
Scientists from the University of California in Berkeley argue that if Walmart raises an average salary to $ 12 per an hour, it will cost corporations $ 3.2 billion.
This is about 1.1% more than she spends on a salary right now.
If Walmart completely shifts the growth of wages on the shoulders of buyers, then each visit to the store will cost them only 46 cents more expensive.
For a year, they will be spending $12.39 more than it does now.
Walmart proponents quite speculate that the protests took place in only nine states and did not cause the corporation any minor damage.
“Chern Friday” lasted in her shops from 8 p.m. on Thursday to midnight of the following day, while Walmart sold about 5000 goods per second.
In total, its cash registers produced almost 100 million transactions on Black Friday.
A spokeswoman of the corporation Dan Fogelman argued in an interview an interview with Huffington’s post that jobs had left “less than one-five” Walmart employees, and that the protest was just another “another PR trouser trouser” trade union.
"A free cash register!"
Propagations continued this week in New York, where they weren't Walmart department stores (they aren't favored in this progressive city, so they're not here yet), but McDonald's and other Cheap Restaurants.
McDonalds says that he sells billions of serving, and after that he doesn't even give you a hospital and doesn't pay you for an honest job!
Jumaine Williams, Member of the New York City Council
At the moment, the minimum wage, according to federal and New York laws, is $ 7.25 an hour.
Fast-tricidance restaurants increase, but not so much, and on average, their ordinary workers receive $8.90 per hour in New York.
There is no one who earns less of them in this expensive city.
How can you exist in New York with such money is incomprehensible to me.
At one time, almost a quarter of American teenagers passed through the McDonalds, who worked for their sheaves after school, while living with their parents.
Few people saw in this the main source of supremacy or planned to stay there for a long time.
Now I'm constantly looking forward to an interview with McDonald workers, who are complaining that they must live on this salary and sometimes even fdour children for it.
On the other hand, there is a comment in the Wall Street Journal forum, the author of which notices irresponsibly to start children if you don’t know what you’re going to nurture them.
Protesters, who began at 6:30 a.m. on Thursday at McDonald's 40th street and Madison Avenue, demanded that cashiers and chefs start paying at least $ 15 an hour, more than doubling their current minimum earnings.
They also demanded that trade union organizations be created in the field of fast-feeding.
U.S. law prohibits the administration from dismantivating and punishing trade union activists with quibble and redundancies.
On the other hand, the administration sparsely facilitates their lives.
But it is also difficult to speculate by a trade union of rapid food for objective reasons.
one of the most important - the extraordinary turnover of personnel.
Disagree
Noisy protests took place on this day and in a number of other Cheap restaurants of Manhattan.
Culmination of the action was in the middle of the day a rally with McDonald’s next to the Times Square, in which several local Democratic politicians spoke, in particular Jumeine Williams, a member of the City Council Jumaine Williams, who said: “McDonalds says that it sells billions of serving, and after that he does not even give you sick and doesn’t pay you for an honest job!”
The demonstrators were also supported by other eminent New York-democrats, such as the mayor, Bill De Blasio, who said that “we must amicably join in support of fast-growing workers in order to achieve a fair pay and economic well-feed that every New Yorker deserves!”
As the New York Times described what happened, it was the largest action of this kind in the history of the American fast food sector.
But it was attended by only a few hundred people, moreover, not all of them were employees of a quick catering, in which tens of thousands of people are employed in New York.
It is not yet clear whether this spark will give birth to the flame of a mass movement.
"While cheating the brain turns out to be an average"
Popular among the fans of modern technologies the topic of augmented reality in recent years it is customary to perceive mainly through the prism of special glasses.
At the first in the summer at its annual conference the current model showed Google, and then in November it became known that the application for the corresponding patent was filed by Microsoft.
However, from the conversation with the head of the group of interactive 3D-technologies in the Cambridge laboratory of Microsoft, Sharam Isadia it follows that for the scientists of this company glasses is a passed stage.
They are accompanied by the prospect of manipulating virtual objects in the air with bare hands, the creation of virtual outdoor spaces.
- Please tell us what your research team is doing in general?
- We are engaged in the interaction of a person with the machines, but at the same time we are trying to expand the boundaries of this interaction.
So far, humanity in the mass is stuck at the stage of working with pixels on a flat screen, sometimes poking them with a toe with a toe.
We want to look 5-10 years ahead and anticipate cardinal changes in this interaction.
For example, the Xbox and Kinect sensors are a step forward, and almost not a single Xbox today without Kinect is not sold, because everyone is interested in gesture control.
- What's still ahead of us?
- Despite the fact that Kinect has transferred the interaction to the physical level, a lot still takes place on a flat-screen, sometimes in 3D.
The input of information has improved (the system receives more than the data), and the output is still not very.
We are trying to change this, we are working on a truly three-dimensional display systems based on a variety of technologies, including projective ones.
You need to let the computer world out into our, physically, make it more tangible.
To do this, however, you need to recognize not only the user, but also the space around him.
Then we will be able to supplement the real world with virtual objects in a much more convenient form.
And first of all - to hell these silly helmets of virtual reality!
- What do you think about voice control?
It’s a popular thing, but doesn’t they overestimate it?
- It definitely can not be called a panacea - the question of privacy arises, because you do not always want to notify others about their actions and intent.
In fact, all types of interactions with computers are good, but each in its own niche.
For example, in order to control devices in public places, we had a project in which we thought of gestures, but not on sweeping, but on such stingy, short.
In this case, the gestures were filmed not by the camera of the device, but with a wrist bracelet, which recorded the movements of the bones and muscles.
While it is large, but theoretically it can be reduced to the size of the wristwatch.
But in general, the future belongs to the mixed type of management, for example, a gesture + a voice.
- It's like this?
- For example, how do you ask me to give this soda jar?
You’ll tell me and show it at the Same time.
- I'm usually just saying.
- It's going to be very hard to recognize.
- So do you want to force users to adapt to what a machine can or can't at the moment?
It doesn’t have to, but it’s a rapprochement.
I think in the near future we mainly will have to work on the development of new types of sensors, which will more accurately determine a person's reactions.
This can be, for example, laser sensors, they give a good resolution in depth, this is very important.
- If we talk about your work with Xbox Kinect Sensors, what complaints do you have about modern cameras?
Isn’t there a lack of resolution, depth or anything else?
- In principle, the current generation - this is what can be relied in our work on three-dimensional recognition.
Of course, it would be good to receive megapixels 8 at a rate of 1000 k / s.
But it's not about the megapixels themselves, it's a matrice and depth.
From the latter point of view, all the current technologies for us are not good enough for us - this adds work to the designers of algorithms.
That is, you need to keep in mind not only about permission according to the axes of the X and Y, but also Z.
Also very important is the speed, the number of frames per second.
The movements of a person are quite dynamic, and the current 30 k/s is frankly frankly, especially for gestures.
Steven Betishes from our Radmond laboratory made touch sensor with an adjustable terrified machining lattice - from 1 to 100 ms, with modern serial sensors closer to the second (60-100) indicator.
Not everyone realizes how this affects the interaction of man and machines.
In my work, I would very much useful the equivalent of such a device, only does not require a touch, so that the frames per second would have a lot.
Don’t have to enlargize the number of cameras?
Kinect now has three "cameras", one of which is actually an infrared emitter, and the second - a receiver of the reflected signal.
But now the third camera - in fact, the usual sensor of the visible range.
It does not be used to determine the depth of the object.
Potentially more cameras could solve a problem.
Aggravate it, increasing the required volume of calculations.
It would be a good way to create a Kinect flexible analogue, to play with the curvature location of the cameras and see how this will help in a three-dimensional position determination.
- As far as I remember, Microsoft, unlike Google, did not present its glasses to the public.
Don’t you think that from the point of point of point of view of the everyday use of augmented reality technologies, glasses is one of the most promising platforms?
- Of course, walking all the time, keeping the smartphone in raised hands is not very convenient, but here's what I'm thinking: the steadfast option would be "transparent" augmented reality, when on the basis of a cloud platform you could sneak from glasses to a smartphone, a projection display and further everywhere.
Botties are a very personal device, this is their strength (private things see only you), and at the same time the weakness - augmented reality based on glasses will not allow you to work on virtual objects together with other people.
Let's imagine for a moment that manipulating virtual ulographic objects in the air is available not only Tony Stark from the "Iron Man", but also a measurable mortal.
There’s a single problem that critics of such an idea often point to: there’s no tactital withdrawal!
My hands don't feel anything!
What are the answers of your group preparing for this challenge?
- In my Lectures, I often say that an augmented reality is the seventh breakthrough in the interaction of a person and a machine.
I think the eighth may well be the addition of tactile sensations.
In the meantime, one of the interesting steep tricks - the use of the second hand as a kind of substrate for the image.
It's a great job of pressing!
But there are also technologies, which are really aimed at dividing these “pictures in the air in the air” – for example, the interference of several directed ultrasonic rays at a certain point where the finger is located gives a feeling, but so far it is very weak - as if you were blew on your pilgrims.
There are also bracelets on wrists, which affect the nerve endings in the fingers - also a promising direction.
Have you tried to deceive your brain?
To force him to think that he can feel what he seems to feel at the moment when he sees something?
It’s a good idea, we haven’t tried it yet.
Here there is another task, which will have to be beaten for a long time - how to force a person who is physically located in a very limited space, to believe that he is going through an open, almost infinite - we are also working on the concepts of treadmill (not at all such as in clubs), moving platforms, as well as huge balls.
While deceiving the brain turns out to be an intermediary, work for many other years.
That's what makes the work on a virtual reality so appealing to a researcher - many things in an absolutely infringed state.
