The head of the Main Control and Verification Directorate: The Schwarz-System test failed due to the fault of the politicians.
In the industry of public procurement, the Czech Republic owns good control bodies and, despite the availability of high-quality legislation, lags behind in its application.
This is the statement of Miroslav Kahl, vice-president of the supreme control and audit department, from an interview for Aktuálně.cz.
“The law will never be perfect, but it should be applied correctly, which, in my opinion, we do not have enough,” Kala commented.
Such conclusions are provided by a joint inspection of the Czech and German control bodies.
As an exemplary incorrect practice, the approach of Peter Nechas to the so-called quartz-sie-secret system.
Recently the Prime Minister said that GDPP (Civil Democratic Party) will not bother entrepreneurs with checks - is it prohibited or allowed?
“The law should be in any way established, and, if it prohibits something, the prime minister cannot withstand the work of the institutions, which are obliged to check and achieve the fulfillment of their obligations,” Kala says.
At the meeting of the Audit Committee in the Chamber of Deputies you talked about a joint project between the Czech and German sides, within the framework of which legislation on public procurement of both countries was compared.
What exactly were we talking about?
This refers to the parallel test, which we started probably two years ago.
To put it more simply that European legislation approves how public procurement should be carried out, followed by national legislation, followed by national legislation and then also applied in practice.
All the information was collected and, even with an unfinished inspection, interesting differences have been revealed - in other words, the legislation can be "accuracial and unterroxicedant", but in the practice of application we are inferior in some parameters to Germany.
From this it it follows that it is not enough to create an increasingly and more updated rules, it is necessary to focus on the application of the law itself.
And in fact, how can this project help you, what it means to you?
It is such a joint control that can contribute to efforts to stop clarifying the laws, reducing and improving the restrictions, because this does not lead to a positive result.
An economy means getting an appropriate thing for a sensible (but not always low) price to avoid an enrichment and, possibly, criminal behavior.
But it is not the fact that we will achieve this, reducing the restriction of orders.
Perhaps this system will be overwhelmed with "paper" so that those who wish to find loopholes in a given system will find them much more easily than if the restriction does not decrease.
You are talking about the internal problems of the practical application of legislation on public procurement.
And how does the control system operate in Germany?
Does there such an organization as our KRU (Deputy-reaissance management) or does it work differently?
As for the institutions, Bundesschнungshof, arranged according to our principle, there is also a collegium that works in a slightly different way, but mainly both institutions function in the same way.
In addition, the powers are to a certain extent similar, but since Germany has a federal structure, then there are audit divisions at the level of the federal Länder - their system is somewhat different from our.
BRH can manage only federal money, we have public funds.
Public funds in our regions and municipalities are supervised at the level of Federal Accounting Chambers.
As far as the local legislation, is it easier there than we have?
In general, I wouldn’t want to compare without specific data, but in some respects Germany can be taken as a specimen, but we can’t definitely say that it is better in everything.
It's so, so because they're better able to cope with the control?
Definitely not, but I repeat, I don't like such comparisons.
But I must say that even exemplary countries face many problems.
Otherwise they wouldn’t have benefited from working with our office, right?
If we talk about domestic legislation, how did the amendments to the Public Procedurial Law affect your institution, somehow the consequences are manifested?
Given that the legal force of these amendments is quite short, it has not yet manifested itself in our control work.
Since we carry out control on the fact, it is necessary to take into account a certain delay.
We take this into account only in the process of preparation of future inspections - we began to use our new tool "detecting the risk of unfavourability", through which we chafted almost 14000 state contracts. We have analysed them. There, of course, these changes will be manifested, because restrictions are changing, the conditions for certain types of tenders are changing and so on.
What do you do you think the approving of this law will benefit or an additional burden on bureaucracy?
I believe that this law, of course, a step in the right direction, and, I, I hope, it will be confirmed.
The problem, which may arise, it is such that the rules will “tighten” and they will be impossible to comply with them.
In the operation of previous regulations, controlled enterprises are associated with the supplier (e.g., regional operating programmes with the regional council) that any non-obligation of the Public Purchasing Act constitutes a violation of budgetary discipline.
In such a case, it is worth tightening the law in such a way?
I don't think this way.
The system must denigrate those who want to seize and abuse, but not to punish those who make a formal mistake that does not affect the decision.
Such a system will only create even greater pressure on bureaucracy.
And so how to get out of this situation?
Let's be aware that this route will not lead anywhere.
Recently, the prime minister said that the GDP will not bother entrepreneurs with checking the so-called quartz system.
"Shvarz system" is prohibited or allowed?
The law should be in any manner established, and, if it prohibits something, the Prime Minister cannot resist the work of the bodies charged with verifying and achieving the fulfilment of their obligations.
We can say: “We will change this law and solve it”, but we can’t say that let’s pretend that nothing is happening.
The law on public procurement contains quite strict rules, as and what should be formally carried out, and this is correct. This will protect public procurement.
But, on the other hand, it is terracticant, when the candidate with the best offer is disqualified only because of formal deficiencies.
The law will never be perfect, but it should be applied correctly, which, in my opinion, we are lacking.
