MVRDV architects prove that real adventures come not only in the imagination - the example of Spikenisis and its newly built Book Hill - 2 photos
“It seems to me this building, it looks futuristic, at last we can see something interesting again,” says Lizette Verheig, a passer-by on the edge of the road.
And Stefan Stéchurson, an IT specialist at a nearby large-scale enterprise, said: "No, this building is really beautiful."
But I'm asking myself: why do we need another library today?
They all all have the internet, iPad and e-books.
Now no one voluntarily goes to such old-fashioned libraries, right?
Spikenis, an unprincipated sleeping town near Rotterdam, owns an unusual record.
Its population of 80,000 has the lowest level of education in the Netherlands.
To combat this annoxious circumstance a few years a few years ago it was decided to contribute to general education and to build seven fictitious bridges, which are depicted on euro banknotes, in the form of screeparated miniatures made of reinforced convoy.
The success of this educational initiative was modest.
And then the fathers of the city realized that they still have the only opportunity to overcome the statistics: to build a library here!
Vinie Maas from the Rotterdam architectural bureau MVRDV, a master of bold column diarrhal diagrams and the author of witty witty, sometimes even cynical buildings, reacted to the task with his usual equanimity and appeared at competitive listening in in 2003 with five books under the aperture and smear on his face.
And while the members of the jury puzzled and shrugged his shoulders stabbing, the daring Maas folded from his original gift a pyramid in size and completed a speech in defense of his project with the following with the following words: "Dear citizens!"
Here it is my proposition: "Book Mountain" in Spikenis, the so-called Bukenberg!
After nine years, this mountain valued worth 30 million euros has risen.
It is part of the revitalization project, which also includes an underground garage, a supermarket, an e-mail, mail and a number of adjacent houses and townhouses for a total of 50 apartments.
In the beginning of November "Book Mountain" was awarded the second prize of the competition "Best Library of the Netherlands in 2012".
Also, the project participates in the national Dutch competition of wooden structures in 2012.
Thus, a facet suburb, which before could not offer anything but a postmodern pedestrian zone and a surprisingly ugly town hall, whose white facades suggest the idea of a dairy plant has become richer for one modern work of architecture.
But the most important thing - in Spikenises there was the first in the history of this town a public building for cultural purpose.
A Long Road to a Book
First Impression: Eldorado book under the hood for the cheese.
There is only one elevator that takes place in the center of the mountain range, because real fans of architecture and readers will prefer to explore this area on foot.
Gluffed from all sides the room - bright and easily foreseeable, flank flanks and elegant street lanterns unmistakably create a sense of a public urban area.
The urban turmoil recreated perfectly.
The look unwittingly looks for benches, dogs and playing football boys and girls.
And everywhere - books, books, books.
“Usually book shelves stand along the fabulous facades, and in the middle there is a large dark hall, as a rule, uncomfortable and faceless,” says Vini Maas.
We put the classical configuration of the space on your head and pulled the zone for reading from the from the inside to the outside.
The internal space of the book mountain was used skillfully: in the center there are offices, an online library, a chess club, an environmental center and technical premises.
Particularly pleased with the black book shelves, which serve the decoration of the walls, then paraptists, then stairwells.
Their appearance, texture and survamp are unusual.
Even stecombustiated architects and civil engineers are flawed in confucedes, what kind of unidentified material.
“We wanted to work with the secondary materials,” Explains Jop Trowbrest, the project manager representing Spikenisse community, in accordance with the requirements of the standard.
And somehow at one agricultural enterprise in Freisland we met a suitable by-product of agriculture.
For many years in the greenhouses and on the margins in the Netherlands, an artificial fibrous material of a millimeter thick is used as a lining for its roots.
It’s cheap and saves time.
Subtle textiles serve one or two seasons, after that it can be discarded.
For the liquefigeal, this is a cloth - for the first time in such volumes - was commensurate in a panel with a thickness of four centimeters.
under the influence of heat and pressure, the so-called landbaupapastic (KLP) turns into a dark, homogeneous, durable material by a odor slightly resembling a new car and a little sneering.
105 stairs lead to the top.
The one who overcame almost 500 meter-largest route will be rewarded in a literary café not only a fantastic view of the city, but also Dutch croquets and tree ficus in pots.
They take care of the atmosphere, and first of all about the correct humidity of the air on the literary mountain.
