But then Berlin abstinized during the vote.
- Why ?
Stefen Szabo: Germany does not support the actions of the Israelis in Gaza.
Now Germany needs to be wary in connection with its special relations with Israel.
But at the same time I don’t believe that she also does not support an American position.
Germany wanted to demonstrate its independence - without speaking too crutically towards Israel.
During the uprising in Libya in March 2011, Germany also abstained from voting on the introduction of a no-floating zone.
As a result, NATO carried out this proposal.
Is it difficult for Germany to take a clear stipend on complex international issues?
Yeah, that's it.
the fact is that it is in the process of transforming its foreign policy, namely moving away from a policy that, so to speak, was governed by the United States to German foreign policy.
Additionally, the situation complicates the fact that there is no agreed unified policy among Europeans.
Thus, the Germans again find themselves between the two fronts.
It’s expected that they will play a more independent role, but they are not accustomed to.
I believe that so far they are only groping their role, but are on the way to a “harserver” foreign policy.
Such a foreign policy, which France or the United Great Britain conducts.
What does “normal” foreign policy mean from a German point of view?
This is a willingness to take a position in their national interests, not dependent on the position of the United States or European partners.
I believe that German foreign policy depends on economic policies, that is, on exports and ties with certain regions, such as Russia, China or the Middle East.
The economic interests of Germany are largely different from the interests of other large states, and therefore Germany must protect its interests.
Did these economic interests have influencible the position in the Middle Eastern conflict and the vote in the UN?
Firstly, Germany has large markets in the Middle East, in particular in the Gulf states.
Therefore, it needs to exercise caution in order not to offend the public and the elites in the Arab countries.
In any case, it plays a role.
I wouldn’t attach too much importance to that, it’s not a completely one-sided connection.
But in any case, this plays an important role in the settlements of the Germans.
Did Germany harm its relations with the U.S. abstinence in making important decisions, such as a vote on Palestine?
I am sure that in Europe and even in the United States, the understanding of the German position prevails in Europe and even in the United States.
Therefore, I don’t think it was just as dramatic awkward as the Libyan issue.
It is possible that Germany will even acquire a certain respect in this way.
After all, this signals that it should be perceived seriously as a player in the international arena and that its interests should be taken into account.
There are different opinions in Europe about the Palestinian initiative.
On the other hand, the U.S. spoke clearly in favor of veto.
Is there a discrepancy of opinions between the United States and many European states?
By virtue of American domestic politics, such differences have always existed.
“I believe that the Obama government has a sufficient understanding of the European stipulus.
But in connection with the local political situation, the government, of course, can not openly occupy such a position.
I believe that significant differences of opinion are not at all so significant, as it always seems.
If you look at relations between Obama and Prime Minister Netanyahu, you will notice that Obama isn’t at all enthusiastic about Netanyahu’s policy.
Is it difficult for Germany to combine their close ties with Israel and the United States on the one hand and the position of an important partner in the EU on the other?
I think that’s exactly what’s so difficult for the Germans.
Of course, it wouldn’t be much easier for the Germans if there was a unified, contradictory European policy, but it’s not right now.
Therefore, they can not act as part of a broader approach and are forced instead to act, relying only on their own vision of the situation.
That’s exactly what they do with the euro.
I hope that in the future Germany will take a leading role in the development of a common European position.
It is understandable that for Germany this is not easy, at least because of ties with Israel.
This topic has always been clavigged.
But I don't think it is clear to the Germans that they should play a more independent role.
if Germany sees itself as an important international gambler - won't it really take the main role?
Or is the leading role still difficult given to Germany?
Germany is not yet accustomed to this, she is still always unpleasant and, for obvious reasons, it is always difficult to play a significant role.
We will consider, for example, the crisis of the euro - every time Germany assumes an important role, a variety of anti-German feelings are manifested.
It’s not easy for the Germans to deal with it.
In fact, it’s an old problem: you don’t want to be surrounded by hostile countries.
From this point of view, Germany is in a much more difficult situation than the United States.
She should take into account a wide variety of neighbors and opinions, and this is not easy.
The influence of the U.S. in European Politics is constantly weakening, but at the moment the EU does not fill this vacuum, who then remains?
The Germans simply have to play a bigger role.
Even if you don’t like it, it’s frustrating or unlovying for you – that’s life!
