Facts About Sexually Transmitted Vacillants
What are the most important sexually transmitted diseases?
Bacterial STIs include syphilis, chlamydia and trypper (gonorrhea).
Frequent viral STI are HIV, human papilloma viruses, genital herpes and hepatitis.
Pubic pediculosis and scabies belong to parasitic SIPP.
What are the main affected groups?
Syphilis and gonorrhoea occur mainly in men who have sex with men.
The Robert Koch Institute proceeds from the fact that at least one in every five syphilis cases registered in Germany transmission occurs as a result of sexual encounters between men.
Hetero-sysexual adults have frequent sexually transmitted diseases include chlamydial, trichomoniasis, candidiasis (fung), gonorrhoea, and human papillomavirus.
The prevalence of HIV among heterogeneous adults we are relatively low - however, about 20 percent of new HIV infections occur in this group.
Chlamydial infections among young people are much more likely than among other groups of the population.
According to European data, three quarters of all infections affiliate for young people between the ages of 15 and 25.
Human papilloma-pillus also often occurs among young people.
How does the number of cases of influencing changes?
Not all diseases sexually transmitted are subject to mandatory registration.
Syphyphilis incidence, according to the Robert Koch Institute, more than doubled from 1,1697 cases in 2001 to 3,698 cases in 2011.
The number of new HIV infections has been declining since 2007.
In 2011, there were about 2700 cases.
This is about one ten times less than in the previous year.
What Symptoms Describe Venereal Implications
With infectious diseases, ulcers can occur in the genital area, complaints of urinary urination, excretion, lower abdominal abdominal pain, as well as vesicles or seals.
However, often they are not accompanied by pain and other symptoms and go unnoticed.
How do you can protect yourself?
Confections are able to reduce the risk of infection, but do not give one hundred percent protection.
After all, partially causative agents of sexually transmitted diseases, can also be disseminated through a contact infection and with close physical contact.
Therefore, the experts recommend a regular examination, especially with a frequent replacement of sexual partners.
With an early diagnosaltion, it is possible to heal most SIPPs can be cured and long-term consequences can be eliminated.
