The word “Human rights” does not exist in the Constitution
The revolution has returned to Cairo.
Rivating demonstrations in Cairo testify to a deep schism of the country.
The future constitution, based on Shariah, met a furious rebuff.
The Egyptian president didn't smuggle into pathos.
We have to create a transition.
“And his success rests on my responsibility to the people and before God,” he said on state-of-the-art television.
His Speech was addressed to the whole of the people, but primarily to Christian copts, liberals, enlightened Muslims and unreligious Egyps.
After all, all of them, still unreasonably disunittivated in the confused Opposition, are afraid.
Freak out of the religious state on the Nile at the mercy of the mighty “Muslim Brotherhood”.
Mohammad Morsi, almost an apologizing, said that he temporarily cursed the competencies of the constitutional court and expanded his competence “to save the revolution”.
However, what 61-year-old engineer really wants to save the 61-year-old, who holds a doctorate degree from the American University of Southern California, it is not quite clear to the Egyptians and all over the world.
Will judicial power be devoured?
Influentially, 234 articles were dragged in a nocturnal 15-hour slump with obstacles to an Islamist-minded constitutional assembly of 100 participants, and this partly raises fears.
As in the previous constitutions, the draft states that judicial proceedings are based on “principles of Islamic law”.
But what do "principles" mean?
This was and remains a matter of interpreting, and we can be afraid that the Islamists will use the vague wording and the legal outlet arising in connection with it for a stricter interpretation of Shariah.
At the very least, one new article hinted at this: in all matters concerning Shariat, Al-Ashar University, the most important Islamist institution of the country, which enjoys great prestige throughout Sunni Islam, should be involved.
This should not, but it may mean that the clergy overshadows the law, that the de facto would result in the restriction of the judiciary.
In the draft of the Constitution, there is a matter of interpretation
Another problem: the subjudgment of civilians must be preserved to the military court.
These courts during Mubarak’s government served to suppress the opposition.
After the depletion of the Dictator, there were up to 11.000 civilians.
Also, as stated in the project, the state should protect the “true essence of the Egyptian family” and “to maintain its morality and values.”
Legally, this is formulated so fusily that state institutions with the help of this article will be able to control even the content of cinema and literature.
In an open text, this means nothing but censorship.
In general, not a single article establishes directly the equality of men and women.
But another forbade to offend and denigrate the prophet Muhammad and his messengers.
But what is an insult and how it should be punished, remains unclear.
Doubtful also seems the wording that “insulting people” is prohibited.
Will there be enough caricature against the president or a joke about a juridden?
The issue of the Interpretation, as well as much in this project, which Morsi offers for the capitation and which, in his words, “very soon” will be presented to the Egyptians for a referendum.
"Revolution has returned"
For several weeks the opposition has been shaping against the supredominance of the Islamists.
Thousands of thousands of people have gathered in an unusual unanimity on Friday evening at Tahrier Square in Cairo and sworn to destroy this document even before it could come into force.
“Revolution has returned, and we’re going to win,” said Hamdin Sabbakhhi, who took third place in the presidential election.
Laureate of the Nobel Peace Prize and the former head of the International Atomic Energy Department, Mohammad El-Baradei, said that the draft constitution is a place “in the landfill of history”.
Visadier of the short Twitter service, he accused Morsi's adherents of wanting to lead a “putch against democracy”.
“If he declares a referendum, we will go to his palace and throw it away,” said Opposition leader Yasser Said.
“We are not yet tired, the blood of our brothers hasn’t been approximated yet”, – quoted the Egyptian media opposition politician Khaled Aili.
Many of the judges also said they didn’t want to overwhelm the referendum, which would invalidate him.
Quran is our constitution
Well-organized "Muslim Brotherhood", in turn, called for the opposite demonstration, but, as a precaution, not in Tahrir Square, but after mass prayer on the other side of the Nile in front of the Cairo University.
It was attended by a large number of veils and adherent adherents of Salafists, who shouted: "The people demand the application of God's law."
They demanded from Morsi: “Cleard the country!” and assured: “Coronar is our constitution”.
The struggle for power over the symbolic square, on which everything all began, provoked the state of almost a civil war.
Morsi’s follow-grandfathers obviously didn’t want that risk.
"Muslim Brotherhood" said that both adversaries and proponents of the draft constitution spoke loud and clear.
Now it’s the right time to let the people decide at the electoral urn, in what direction the country should go.
The majority for the project of the Islamists, it is considered to be a resolute case.
“Human-Human rights has never been mentioned.”
Hafiz Abu Saeda is furious from this forced constitutional process, which, in fact, was supposed to last until February with the participation of all social forces.
The 48-year-old human rights lawyer and the President of the Egyptian Human Rights Organization (EOPR) defended “Muslim Brotherhood”, when under Mubarak they stayed in prison or were brought to justice.
Not because he divided their worldview, but because human rights are indivisible to him.
For this, he was beaten, sentenced, arrested.
And now the words "human rights" are never encountered in the new constitution, he correlates in an interview with "Welt am Sonntag".
Enlarged Morsi’s influence on all three branches of government is frustrating a lawyer.
These measures are obvious blows to the democratic rules of the game and lead the Egyptians to a new ditceptor.
“Instead of extinguishing the civilian state, the president de facto annulted it,” Saeda said.
After all, without civilian public organizations, democracy cannot function.
Saeda feels that everyone has left him, even an international community that is looking at the ideological struggle of trends on the Nile with a mixture of curiosity and tension.
There may be a recostance for this.
Demonstrator in Tahrir Square warns: "You are creating a monster you will not be able to control."
Norwegian ractirisk: the most suffocating fish in the world?
