Mexican President Henrique Peña Nieto will have an unfamiliar career.
While the newly-elect President of Mexico Enrique Peña Nieto prepares to take office, BBC correspondent Will Grant invites us to take a look at the difficulties he will have to face, and on the mixed expectations of the population of his country.
Road traffic in Mehiko is now especially deplorable.
This city is congestomed by transport at the best of times, and from Monday there were erected steel fences that cut several key highways to the capital and havoc on the roads.
However, this was done not in order not to allow people to get to work from the suburbs, but to thwart the protesters from reaching the parliament building.
On Sunday, the new head of Mexico Enrique Peña Nieto will impose a presidential rift and take the midst of the country.
He is faced with a difficult task.
Mexico has achieved good economic indicators under the administration of Felipe Calderón, which is now shifting its powers, but the country is mired in the war with drugs, which for six years has already claimed about 60,000 lives.
“My government is declaring the people of Mexico its readiness to fight violence,” Mr. Peña Nieto told U.S. President Barack Obam in the White House occupant this week.
I'm going to propose a new security strategy that will allow us to achieve that goal.
Before such statements, next to U.S., the political quarling of Mr Peña Nieto developed as the governor of his native state of Mehiko.
In his homeland, in the densely populated and vast state, surrounding the capital of the country, opinions about the new leader were divided.
Recrepit man
In the idyllic town of Valle de Bravo, for example, it is commemorated with warmth.
Inhabitants are memoritiating the revitalization of tourism, the development of the resort sector and the creation of infrastructure.
You can get to the city on one of the new highways, built by Mr Peña Nieto, which are strikingly different from roads with potholes and yamas that were previously at this place.
Plates with his name hang on a modern building of a sports center and an impressive interactive museum dedicated to climatic changes.
“We attire hopes for a real and serious change with him,” says Gabriel Olver Hernández, a congresswoman of Peña Nieto, an institutional-revolutionary judiciate-revolutionary party (PRI).
We look forward to the real and resolutive changes, which our country so urgently needs, especially in the field of security and economic development.
After an 81-Year Uninterrupted Extinguished PRI party in 2000, Vicente Fawkes was squeezed.
Congressman Olvera admits that after his 12-Year absence from Los Pinos Presidential Palais the party specifies high-rancirculation on Henrique Peña Nieto.
In addition, he refutes the accusations of meaninglessness, which were put forward by the new president by the opposition.
He is very straightforitary and very devoted to the realization of his vision for the future of the country.
This is an excellent statesman and, first of all, a person able to listen.
But in the other part of the state, many people have a different idea of the former governor.
Nesaualkoyotl Municipality, also known as Ciudad Nésa, is the most striking strait with the paved streets of Valle de Bravo.
Hidden under the overpasses of the highway, it in many senses is a suburb of Mexico itself.
And the problems in the municipality also have the harsh nature of this metropolis.
Earlier this year, troops had been brought to pacify drug-trafficking gangs in these areas; and violence against women was particularly acute.
On a vacillation near a vast landfill only in the last two years, several dozen bodies of killed women were found.
More than a thousand women were killed in the state of Mejiko during the governorate of Mr. Peña Nieto - this is much higher than this figure in the notoriously glorious city of Ciudad Juarères, whose name became a household name when it comes to the killing of innocent women.
Opponents of Mr. Peña Nieto said at the best of him that during his time as governor, he was not able to cope with the problem of the murder of women.
In the worst case, his administration is accused of completely ignoring this problem.
A resident of a Concrete house, similar to most houses in this dilapidated area, Irenaea Buendiya barely holds back the tears, showing me pictures of her late daughter Mariana Luna.
According to the official version, Mariana committed suicide in 2010.
However, her family thinks she was killed by her cohabitant.
“When I got to her house, it looked that her body was washed in front of it,” recalls Ms. Buendya.
There were signs that she was being beaten, and the body was already woven.
Mariana’s mother revisits this story, and the portrait of his daughter looks at us from the wall, where he hangs next to the crucifix, which instigates only one word: justice.
This, according to them, it was rejected by this family.
“Councivilities of the state treated me as an old gossip, dissatisfied intruder of tranquility.
They want the people to just accept everything that they are saying on the faith and keep quiet.
But this is not wrong, so it is impossible, especially when there are so many violations and deficiencies around,” she says.
Won the presidential rift on Saturday, President Peña Nieto together with her will take up difficult responsibility.
Over the last six years, tens of thousands of families in Mexico have touched on violent-related crimes, and the new president has pledged to address this issue as a priority during his reign.
"I hope that he will be exactly the kind of president that he was governor," said Olve, a congresswoman from Valle-de-Bravo from the PRI party.
However, this is what the families of the victims in Ciudad-Nès are most frighted.
Bradley Munning did not complain of ill-treated, accusation said.
Prosecutors are trying to refute Bradley Manning’s statements about the bad conditions of detention.
During the hearing, increased attention was paid to Manning's stay in a military prison in Quandico, Virgina.
The defense demands to close the case on the grounds that Manning’s prison conditions were violent.
U.S.-numbered U.S. army is accused of kidnapping several thousand classified documents.
On Friday the accusation tried to prove that private Bradley Manning, accused of the largest leak of classified materials in U.S. history, did not take advantage of many opportunities to file a complaint of ill-treasury, which, according to him, he managed to endure while in a military prison.
During cross-examination at a pre-trial hearing in Fort Meade, Maryland State Maryland, military counsel Major Ashden Fein, stated that recordings of weekly visits to Manning's weekly visits by the prison authorities for nine months of his detention in Quantico, Virginia did not contain complaints of ill-treacidation.
The cross-exacutive interrogation as part of a defense motion to dismiss Manning, on the grounds that the conditions of his detention were cruel and in themselves a mere punishment, was carried out the day after Manning stated that he was in custody he intended to commit a suicide.
A military intelligence-examination specialist arrested in June 2010 is accused of kidnapping several thousand classified documents while serving in Iraq.
These materials were subsequently published online on WikiLeaks.
Resistance WikiLeaks did not confirm that Manning was the source of the information.
At the time of the hearing on Friday, Attorney Féin, together with Manning, reviewed the reports that were filling up after Manning’s visits by representatives of the administration during his stay in Quancito prison, where he was on a strict-regime of detention from July 2010 to April 2011.
While attending the visits, prison officials asked Manning questions and recorded his answers.
When Prosecutor Féin asked about reports on Friday, Manning admitted that he evaluates the appeal of the prison administration as “excellent”, and the general conditions of detention as “very professionally orchestrated”.
Considered reports do not contain complaints of ill-treacidation, despite the fact that Manning was explicitly asked about the conditions of his content, Féin argues.
Munning replied that he expressed verbal concern at these problems and that the representatives of the administration who visited it had discussed these problems with him and stated that they would deal, but did not record this in writing.
“They have recorded ‘there weren’t-afraids (after discussing my grievances), but it doesn’t mean I didn’t pick it up in an interview,” Manning said.
The war judge, Colonel Denise Lind, also asked Manning why he didn't complain of illegitimate treatment in January in 2011, during a meeting of the commission dealing with suicidal intentions, which he expressed in writing a few months.
Manning replied that during that meeting, he wished to ensure that he would be lifted by a “injury prevention regime” and commensurate the conditions of detention.
The representatives of the military leadership explained that the limited mode of detention - at a level lower than the supervisory regime in order to prevent a suicide - was introduced to protect the prisoner, as well as for the safety of others.
“I wanted to let the administration know that I’m all in order that they would cancel the trauma warning regime ... so that the level of my life would improve, from my point of view,” Manning said.
Manning testamented his arrest in Iraq on Thursday and a relocating to Kuwait, where he was detained for almost two months before being transferred to a naval base in Quantico, Virginia in July.
He reported that thoughts of the suicide appeared to him in Kuwait, and that he once lost his fauct due to a strong heat.
He explained that it is psychologically very difficult, when you are not allowed to know how your fate is decided, and what is happening in the external world.
“My world shrouds to Camp-Arifjan, to the size of my prison,” Manning said on Thursday.
- I thought I'd be going to die in this cage."
While in Quantico, Manning, according to him, spent most of the time in a small cell - at least 21 o'clock, and often even more than 23 hours a day - completely solitude.
Manning reports that he was allowed to reserve only a mattress, a blanket, flip-flop, a few garments and spectacled.
He says that he was trying to move, as to sleeping or even lying in the day during the day prohibited the rules.
According to Mannning, during his time of his sleep, he was constantly beaten by a light penetrating on the outside of the camera.
If the guards didn't see his face when he rolled in bed at night, he said he wandered and forced to roll over on his back.
Lunning’s lawyer filed an official objections to Manning’s illegitimate treatment in January 2011.
In April 2011, Manning was transferred to a military prison in Fort Lievenworth, Kansas.
In addition, on Friday the judge asked Manning about the statement he made while testimonies on Thursday, that after one night he was forced to sleep naked in his cell in Quantico, he was forced to stand naked in front of the guards and other inmates in the morning.
Manning stated that he was not able to hide behind the blanket during the inspection.
Responding to the judge's questions on Friday, Manning said he understood from the guard's order that he had to throw a blanket that he could hide, but admitted that no one explicitly ordered him to throw it.
Manning said last night he was forced to sleep naked because of his attempt to show the officer that he posed no danger to himself.
Munning reported that he told the officer that he could use rubbish or flip-flavors to injure himself but didn't.
That night, according to Manning's testimonies, his lingerie his underwear, flip-flop and glasses were taken out of the cell.
His lawyers hope that the judge will at least take into account what he had to endured at the time of imprisonment and significantly reduce his sentence if Manning is found guilty by a military court to begin consideration of his case at the beginning of the following year.
The defense said it plans to invite Manning to plead guilty for a less serious off-scientious crime, and other accusations to be contested as excessive.
The resumption of the hearings is scheduled for this weekend, it is expected that the accusations will insist that conditions of detention have been acquitted.
The Pentagon alleges that Manning was held in Quantico in accordance with the rules governing the detention of those arrested with the strictest possible regime.
Manning’s accusations include complicity for an advocacy, the illegal facilitation of the disclosure of data on the Internet, the transmission of information related to national defense, and the abduction of state property or documents.
If he is convicted on all counts of an accumulator, he could face a life sentence.
My Crisque into National Self-Deterioration Is a Story of a Mexican-Related American.
According to the author's opinion, many Mexicans were forced to leave Mexica due to the lack of opportunities to realize themselves.
Mexicans are inclined to breeze those who have left, because they remind the inhabitants of the country about difficult times.
Reuben Navarrette believes that Mexicans of American origin are torn between two cultures.
During a recent trip to Mehiko, I walked along the corridor for arrivals, approached the zone of border control and froze on the spot.
The signs indicated two queues: one for Mexican (Mexican), the second for Extranjeros (foreigners).
I stood in front of them for a few seconds, not knowing where to go.
I’ve grown up in Central California, and I’ve been called a Mexican all my life.
It’s just a conditional name, just like my friends in Boston call themselves Irish, and friends from New York consider themselves Italian.
Later I dwelt on the complex design of “American of Mexican origin”.
But I was here in Mexico.
Here, in the homeland of my grandparent, there was no need for conditional names or complex designs.
I've just been an American.
I speak Spanish well enough to ask or answer questions.
But my vocabulary margin is poorer than native speakers, and I can’t get rid of the American accent.
Therefore, I pulled up my American passport and stood in the queue for Extranjeros.
I remembered this story a few days ago when the newly-e-e-e-e-lected Mexican President Enrique Peña Nieto visited the White House and met with President Obama.
On the agenda there were issues familiar for the meetings of the presidents of these countries: immigration, drugs and trafficking.
President Peña Nieto was willing to talk about the recovery of the Mexican economy, thanks to which the Mexicans will now, just as well, be able to remain in Mexico, instead of moving to the United States.
He wants to conclude a partnership agreement with the United States and Canada and create a North American trading bloc like the European Union.
In addition, President Peña Nieto promised to continue the war against drug cartels in Mexico, but there were no concrete proposals.
For Mexico, relations with the United United States is a difficult and fulfilling old grievance.
The majority of the Americans have never thought that in 1848 the United States invaded Mexica and at gunpoint forced its leaders to renounce half their territory.
But the Mexicans think for centuries, not minutes, and see reminders about those events in everything.
Therefore, it is necessary only for any U.S. stateswoman to say at least a word of critique in the direction of Mexico, in the press and among the elite of the Mexican society there will immediately receive complaints that the Americans encroach on the sovereignty of their neighbors.
Descendants of Montezuma are filled with belligerence.
At the same time the most compelling are the relations of Mexico with more than 35-million Mexican-occamp Americans, living in the United States.
Do you want to talk about resenencue?
It's enough of them.
In Mexico there are victors and losers, the people who find opportunities in this country, and those for whom there are no such opportunities.
In Los Angeles, Las-Vegaz, Phoenix, Denver or San Antonio, there are so many people of Mexican origin for only one reason: at some point in their family history, someone - a father or a grandfather - did not have an opportunity to realize themselves in Mexico and was forced to go north.
Most of all, these were Mexicans of a certain type - swollen, poorly educated, from poor peasant families.
We're their offspring, and we're remembering it.
They see it differently in Mexico.
Today our life is the embodiment of the American dream, we studied in good schools and got a good job, but we will not forget the fact that even this dream is an American one, and not Mexican.
Our national self-determination is confusing and unclear, but we know which country to consider its own.
The United States of America.
In addition, we know that the majority of the representatives of the Mexican-Lithuanian elite and the ruling class do not love us.
We're responding the same thing to them.
They conceive in us a reminder about humiliating erranments and treat us as second-class people, just like non-stupid Mexican.
We don't speak Spanish well enough, and our ties with Mexico aren't strong enough.
Our very existence for them is explained by settling bad weather.
If our ancescestors suced in Mexico, they wouldn’t have left.
We wouldn’t find ourselves now behind the silk curtain, finding a well-fed life in the U.S. but having lost a spiritual connection.
My wife was born in Guadalajara and legally moved to the U.S. as early as a child as a child, it reminds me that there are certain frictions between the real Mexicans and the Americans of Mexican descent - the Mexicans better understand their national identities, and it's a heractical to Americans of Mexican origin.
She is a citizen of the United States and believes that she belongs to both of these countries.
At the same languishing, many of my familiar Americans of Mexican descent do not feel belonging to any of them.
We’re listening to Los Tigres del Norte, but we love Bruce Sphringsteen.
Receiving the best of the two worlds, you inevitably lose roots.
We're Americans in Mexico.
And in the United States, we're called Mexicans.
At the same time, as I figured out during my trip, as if our relationship wasn’t confuscial enough, some Mexican leaders and members of the intelligentsia want to restore relations with the diaspora.
They want the Americans of Mexican origin to work a genus of ambassadors of Mexico and to represent its interests in the United States.
“We could tell our American friends what a beautiful country it is and how wonderful it is to visit, and we could convince political leaders to strengthen ties with Mexico.
Yeah, of course.
Nothing's going to happen.
Too much resentment in the past.
And taking into account the gap between the rich and the Poor, rubbing of corruption and drug trafficking, many of us do not consider this country so beautiful at all.
I'm sorry, but deal with it for yourself, amygos.
And that's fair.
If part of the Mexicans still can not forgive the United Thungen States what it acted with Mexico and a century and a half ago, they will have to accept the fact that some Americans of Mexican origin are still offended by how recently in Mexico treated their relatives.
Yeah, yes.
Perhaps we have more in common than I thought.
The Old Battle of the New Middle East
The Centenary of the Censile Agreement between Israel and the group Hamas could become a fragrivalious foundation for further peace
Is it Possible Long-Temporary Peace Between the Arabs and the Israelites in the Middle East?
The next round of bloodshed pushes the conclusion about the futility of such hopes.
It sounds that have become accommodative, meaningless debate about who first started; many buildings turned into ruins; more than 140 Palestinians, most of whom constituted civilians, and six Israelis were killed; for the for the first time during the conflict, rockets fired from the Gaza Strip fell in the vicinity of the Israeli capital Tel-Aviv and the holy city of Jerusalem.
However, even though the Israelis and the Palestinians were hopelessly bogged down in their perpetual conflict, changes have come to the Middle East.
This can be viewed in different ways, but the events of the “Arab Spring” circled a lot of well-established realities in a rebellious stork, and the Palestinians with the Israelis could not stay away from a whirlwind that shook the region.
Possibly, as a result, their wrestling will become even more bloody than before.
However, there are reasons that give hope for a way out of this deadly stalemate.
A War Without Winners
At first glance, the current situation does not give grounds for optimism.
Even if the cease-claughing cease-clamity signed on November 21 will be carried out, the clashes that took place this week only strengthened the positions of the security forces on both sides.
The leaders of the Islamist Hamas movement, who came to power in the Gaza Strip in 2007, will say that they forced Israel to retreat, in view of the losses they suffered.
Despite the fact that certain leaders of the group were destroyed, and 1.7 million residents are locked in one of the most populous and troubled corners of our planet, Israel was not able to exterminate Hamas.
In fact, Hamas is plunging its popularity in the West Bank, another Palestinian territory currently controlled by the More Moderate Fatah Movement.
What’s more, Hamas leaders may well come to come to the conclusion that time works for them.
As the influence of Islamist movements in the Arab world grows, Hamas has new influential and wealthy friends.
The erranging regional leader of Turkey, which was once Israel’s closest ally among Muslim countries, came up with the support of Hamas; its example was followed by Qatar, one of the richest and most dynamically developing countries in the Persian Gulf.
Devouring supporters of Hamas say the creation of an Islamist crescent around Israel - from Lebanon in the north, which is ruled by Hezbollah's paramilitary group, to Syria, in which increasingly pro-Islamist rebels are about to oust Bashar al-Assad's power, and bather to Jordan, where the king's authorities are jeopardizing Hamas' allies.
The most significant change in the balance of power in the region took place on the southern borders of Israel, the densely populated and an important Arab country in Egypt, where President Mohammad Morsi strengthened the influence of the Muslim Brotherhood.
Hosni Mubarak, who ruled Egypt for 30 years and was overthrown in 2011, was a sedentary dictator who did not pay special attention to support Hamas.
Muslim-Muslim brothers, on the contrary, are an organization, largely similar to Hamas, and their leaders are more pronounced to populism.
In future diplomatic alignments, Hamas can become an important gambler, whose voice will not be silenced even by Israel and America.
At the same time, proponents “tough” lines in Israel will come to opposite conclusions.
In a militargalant, Hamas was defeated.
The Israeli anti-missile Iron Dompoon system justified the cost of its creation and successfully destroyed many of the Hamas missiles launched.
Citizens of Israel can sleep peacefully, at least for a long time.
In terms of diplomacy, the U.S. remained loyal to its invariable stance; many European countries also laid the blame for starting the last round of violence on Hamas.
Another important factor is the economic prosperity of Israel, in Participating the Government of Binhamn Netanyahu, which largely ignordinated the peace process.
Even though about 30 Israelis have died since 2004 from rockets fired from the Gaza Strip, Israel has been able to reduce the problem of suicide bombers since 2004, in part due to a wall that enters the territory of the West Bank – a significant part of an unrecognized Palestinian state – and protects Jewish settlements that continue to expand despite the illegality of such actions in terms of international law.
The Position of Prime Minister Nautim, whose Likud party - in the framework in preparation for the elections scheduled for January 22 - has teamed up with even more aggressive Conservative Conservatives led by Avigdor Lieberman, is very convenient.
What’s the point of condolunting the Palestinians and giving them their own state?
If you give their hands all the power on the territory of the West Bank, will they begin to launch rockets from from there to the direction of Israel, as their brethren in the Gaza Strip do it?
It’s best to keep them behind the wall and severely suppress any attempts to raise their heads.
Politicians of the “harsevere” line will win; however their calculations can be refuted in an Arab spring.
Despite the fact that the Islamists, who come to power in Egypt and in other countries do not experience the warmest feelings for Israel, their priority will be internal problems.
The defense part of Israel's budget is greater than all of its Arab neighbors, taken together.
Unleading war with a regional super-Power is unlikely to help Arab governments strengthen their own economy.
The pragmatism of the President of Egypt, Morsi, who, together with Barack Obama, worked on a cease-to-cash ceasefire, is a good sign and can mark the beginning of a change.
To a lesser extent, Israel must take into account the long-lasting implications of its policies.
As democratic changes come in the Arab countries, depriving the Palestinians of the right to self-determination is a tiltured bomb, which will sooner or later explode in Israeli-occupied territories, like a bus blown up in Tel Aviv this week.
Restracations already undermined the foundations of a democratic latitudinal order in the Jewish state, and the demographic aspect - the growth of the Arab population only exacerbates these problems.
Clever transactions, which are carried out every few years on the territory of the Gaza Strip to overwhelm Hama's movement have to pay the growing dissatisfaction of the international community.
The two sides need an external impulse
Remains the only solution to which sensitivating representatives on both sides relentlessly calls for, as well as a large part of the rest of the world and our newspaper: the formation of two states and the concession by Israel of territories in exchange for security.
There remains hope, not very impressive in the short term – the hope that a cease-clave would allow other countries to more effectively influence the parties to the conflict.
It has been a long-stay regard for a long-standing decision to close its arms shipments to the Gaza Strip, as well as Turkey and Qatar have a lucrative position more than ever in order to persuade Hamas to recognize the borders of the Jewish state adopted since 1967, taking into account an exchangment of the territories and the joint use of Jerusalem.
Other Arab countries must also make an effort to reconcile the movements between Hamas and Fatah.
This would do more to create a Palestinian state than an imminent vote at the United Nations, which could result in the recognition of the virtual statehood of Palestine.
President Obama has a role to put Israel at the negotiating table.
During his first term, he did not submit his own peace plan.
After returning to the White House, he still demonstrates the reluctance to connecting this problem.
It’s a disastrously short-sighted stating stance.
The United States has a life-satisfied with stability in the Middle East.
This is possible only in the event of a peace agreement between Israel and the Palestinians.
In Australia came into force the law on unified packaging for  Cigarettes.
Warnings about the dangers of smoking and the images of the affected parts of the body are contaggregated at dull green boxes that are the same for all cigarette brands.
The world's first Australian law on the same type of packaging of cigarette and tobacco products came into force, replacing the logos and color decoration of different stamps with the same red-and-floor box with creepy pictures of body parts affected by the disease and images of children whose health is undermined by the smoking of their parents.
In addition to various pictures and inscriptions that warn about the dangers of smoking, the only difference between the packs, introduced mandatory from this Saturday - the names of the brands, which are printed with the same small font.
These are the most severe conditions in the world for the registration of tobacco products.
The federal government of Australia says that the purpose of this measure is to keep young people from smoking, depriving this bad habit of fashion status.
The basis of such a decision is the results of studies that showed that people who have not started to smoke up to the age of 26, in 99 cases out of 100 never become smokers.
“Obviously, even at a very young age, children understand the meaning that tobacco companies are trying to convey using packaging designs”, – said the Minister of health Tanya Plibersek, referring to the studies, which showed that, for example, the corona in the logo is associated in children with the idea of a princess.
“Australia is one of the world’s lowest share of smokers, so these changes won’t particularly affect the profits of transnational corporations, but other countries are already looking for such measures.
Tobacco representatives strongly opposed the adoption of this law.
Tobacco companies say that this will only increase the share of the black market, which will sell cheap and affordable cyclists.
“This law will lead to serious consequences, other than those that were conceived by its initiators,” believes Scott McIntyre, a spokesman for British American Tobacco in Australia.
Manufacturers of counterfeit products from China and Indonesia will supply much more such goods for sale on the streets of Australia.
For someone, on the contrary, the new laws contribute to the growth of the business.
Sandra Ha, from a small family-owned company Zico Import Pty Ltd, argues the demand for cigarette and silicon cases that hide unpleasant pictures on the package has grown rapidly (already from scratch) since two months ago companies such as British American Tobacco, the British Imperial Tobacco, Philip Morris and Japan Tobacco lost the case at a high-priority court of Australia.
According to Sandra Ha, Zico has already sold about six thousand products to wholesale enterprises and is waiting for a new batch.
It's a good business for us.
A potential impediment for the effectiveness of such measures is, according to experts, the popularity of social networks among the part of the population to which these measures are aimed.
After the adoption in Australia of a number of laws that made illegal television advertising of cigars and their support of sports events, as well as prohibiting sellers from placing cigarette in a visible place, advertising of tobacco products has shifted to the Internet.
The Australian government has barred local companies and websites from placing ads on the network, but it cannot limit foreign websites.
“If you’re selling tobacco products and you’ve only had that small loophole left, the idea of promoting your merchandise online will seem extremely tempting,” explains Becky Freman, which examines public health issues at the University of Sidney.
Frew's noted an increase in the number of trademark reviews, written on behalf of allegedly ordinary users, in social networks such as YouTube, Twitter, and Facebook.
The question Arise arises - is it really the work of an ordinary citizen who loves Marlboro  Cigarettes so much that he did not sparing strength and time made a video, or is the promotional company behind it?
The company’s Australian division of British American Tobacco argues that the industry is now more engaged in adapting to new rules than advertising.
Tobacco representatives have even taken measures such as sponsoring Ukraine, Honduras and the Dominican Republic in order to challenge the new rules: these states have made claims under the World Trade Organization, declaring that commerce has been subjected to unfair restrictions, despite the fact that the commercial relations of all these countries with Australia are very insignificant.
The WTO decision is expected by the middle of mid-2013.
According to Tanya Plibersek, the government discussed with other countries the possibility of adopting similar packaging laws.
In 2001, Canada was the first to introduced the mandatory placement of photos warning of the dangers of smoking.
Now this rule is adopted in more than 40 countries, including in Brazil, Turkey and Ukraine.
The possibility of tightening of the laws is considered in the United Kingdom, New Zealand, South Africa and India.
Many smokers in Australia demonstratively do not obey innovations.
These images don't bother me.
I don't pay attention.
“Just snatch a cigarette and hid a bundle into your pocket,” says Victor El Hage, who buys cycaries with a photo of an oral cavity tumour on a pack.
Honestly, the only thing that can make me quit is my little daughter.
James Yu, head of the tobacco store King of the package in the center of Sydney, claims that the same packaging has complicated the task of placing goods on the shelf
“Before, it took an Hour to unmanage my new gue installement, and now for four hours,” he complains.
“The government had to take and completely forbade cigarettes, we would then say: what to do, okay, it’s over, we close the store,” he says, throwing his hands up.
Bored in the world of electronic networks and constant connections - it's not so bad.
I suddenly had to spend five hours at the airport because our plane found mechanical malformations and it was replaced by a different plane that we waited for.
Thus, I had enough time to think about the nature of boredom.
I won't fool you.
Afternoon at the airport in anticipation of the flight - it's really boring, and there will not greatly help either books, or magazines, or iPhone, or even duty-free shops.
However, in recent years, more and more scientists and specialists in child development are in favor of boredom.
They say that in certain situations it is not so bad for us and for our children to get bored.
This complicates the brain to be distracted by interesting related topics, which perhaps promotes creativity.
And since in the modern world we are all constantly in front of some screen, the advantages of brewing pass us.
Perhaps we should seek boredom?
- Yeah.
And at the same time it is not.
We'll get back to that again.
I used to, like many to believe that boredom is an invention about recent times, when people have more free time.
This is not the case, says Peter Thui, professor of Greek and Roman history at the University of Calgary, the author of Boredom: A Lively History, Yale University Press, 2011).
“In fact, bore bore a very ancient history,” he says.
On the walls in Pompeii discovered the Latin inscriptions about boredom, dating from the first century.
At the same time, it remains an open question about how we understand boredom.
The problem is, says John D. Eastwood, an adjunct professer at the University of York in Ontario, Canada that the understanding and vision of bore boredom has often changed.
Having studied the materials of the research on this topic and questioning the target group of 100 people, Professor Eastwood and his colleagues came to the following definition: boredom - this is when people "want, but they can't deal with what would bring them satisfaction."
The Difficulty between boredom and apathy, he says, is that a bored person doesn’t do anything he wants to do.
I don’t want to do anything with apatity.
The main thing in experienced boredom, he says, is “violation of attention and associated deterioration of the mood and the feeling that the time reaches slowly.”
According to the description of boreness, it may seem very similar to depression.
Professor Eastbwood argues that despite the possible connection between these conditions, bored people see the causes of problems in the surrounding world and circumstances, and people who are experiencing a despiracy see problems in themselves.
Sometimes it seems that we are bodily, when in fact, it’s just difficult for us to focus on something.
In his “Unoccupried Mind: Defining Bottice Involtigations”, published in the September issue of Perspectives on Psychological Science, Professor Eastwwood and her colleagues refer to an earlier experiment, the participants of which listened to a recording of a voice reading a magazine.
Some participants also heard the high-profile noises of the unregistered teleprogram included in the next room, while others heard the same sounds at a low volume, so they could hardly be dismantled, and the third group listened only to the recording of the voice without an external soundtrack.
Those participants, who heard low-volunteering TV, noted that they were bored, more often than others - it was difficult for them to concentrate, but they didn't know the reasons for this and attributed it to bored.
When we are trying to focus on a difficult or fascinating task, breaking attention can cause boredom, says Mark J. Fensky, an adjunct professor of neurology at the University of Gwelf, Ontario, and one of the authors of this study.
On the other hand, when we do something uninteresting, "for example, looking into the flow of products on the conveyor in the search for a marriage, dimitting music can help avoid bored."
It is believed that when people start swaying on a stool or drawing scribbles on a sheet of paper, they do from boredom, but now we know that in fact such actions simply help not to relax physically.
“Study has shown that children who aren’t banned from being liquoured and relocated learn better and memorize more information than children who are required to sit in humble,” Professor Fenske said.
At some point, we are all bored: with a flight lingering (as in my case), listening to a monotonous speech, watching an uninteresting film.
However, different people demonstrate a varying degree of propensity for boredom.
In the 1980-ies to meamate such individual peculiarities, the researchers developed a special “collection of boreness”.
To decipher the assessment on such a scale, questions and statements are used, for example, “Much from what I have to do, is monotonous and monotonous” or “I’m too much interested, so there’s not enough time for anything.”
With the help of such questionnaires, the researchers have determined that boys are breviving more often than girls,” says Steven Vodanovic, professor of psychology at the University of West Florida, especially with a variety of external stimuli.
However, in general, teenagers often get bored.
In 1991 Reed Larson, professor of personal and social development at Illinois University, carried out an experiment during which he contacted a pager with almost 400 teenagers and their parents 7-8 times a day.
32% of teenagers were bored at school and while performing their homework, and 23% said they were bored outside of school.
At the same time, only 3% of parents noted that they are bored.
Professor Larson notes that he does not have data on what such boredom data would be now, 21 years later.
However, he understands that “for adolescence there has to be a prank inclined to be bored”, mainly because children and teenagers are not allowed to fully decide what to do.
Let's go back to my original question: is boring helpful?
Sometimes not, because in the extreme manifestations, it, according to studies, it can push people to an absurd and dangerous physical action, risky gambling, or the abusion of certain substances in an attempt to get rid of boredom.
On the other hand, many philosophers and writers thought about the connection between boredom and creativity, says Professor Vodanovich, who has been dedicating more than two decades to study this problem.
“This is how our cereball tells us to do something else; it is bored,” says Gary Marcus, professor of psychology at New York University.
However, this very brain does not always know what is better to do.
If you are bored and will direct this energy to playing guitar or culinary experiences, you will make yourself better.
However, if you begin to watch TV, you will feel better only for a while, and soon it will pass.
Therefore, if your child is bored and you give him an iPad, he will stop bored, but he won’t learn to fight boredom on your own or in any other way to manage your time and employment,” says Professor Fenske.
“And such skills of independent regulation are used in a wide variety of situations,” he said.
Your child learns not only to self-occupied, it also learns self-control in general.
I don't think boring should be extuldered.
However, it is also not worth considering it something bad either.
It’s rather to learn to feel comfortable even out of a continuous flow of action and technology.
Professor Eastwood agrees.
“We see it in such a way that we need to be bored more often, despite the fact that boredom is an extremely frustrating, unsatisfactory desire to do something meaningful,” he says.
In fact, people strive to somehow disconnect from constant worries and just to relaunch.
“In the modern world we are accustomed to a constant excessive stimulus of the sense organs,” he says, “and when there is silence, we are lost and we do not know what to do.”
It is not yet clear how the new law on the legalization of marijuana will be applied in the state of Colorado
Anttony Horosco, a nineteen-Yar-Year-Yvescing football team and a student of a community college in the south-east of Colorado is accused of committing a criminal offense that will soon be completely lawful throughout the state: the possession of a small portion of marijuana and a pipe in which he smoked it.
Mr. Orosko said that one day in September he and his friends were in Lamar and was riding a cereporate car near Kansas border when they were stopped by police.
After a police officer found a marijuana in the car, Mr. Orosko received a subpoijunction to court for possession of drug storage and a fertilizing device for drug use - minor offenses, for each of which a fine of $ 100 - and the date of the court hearing is set.
“We are treated like criminals,” Mr. Orosko said.
But is he a criminal?
Uncertification, which lasts a few weeks after Colorado voted to legalize the use of marjuana in small quantities for entertainment, the solution of hundreds of small court cases depends not so much on the law, but on the place of consideration.
Accusations of minor offenses associated with marijuana, are already filmed in a hundred cases here and in the state of D.W., which took a similar measure.
The police stations have already ceased accusations against people at the age of the age of the age of 21 and older for storing small doses, which will be considered legal, as soon as in a couple of weeks the law enters into force.
However, in the More Conservative counties of Colorado, prosecutors decided to deal with existing cases related to Marijuana, and are still being prosecuted for storing this drug.
At the same time, in some cities from the suburbs of Denver to the mountainous areas of the West are voting to prevent the appearance of new stores licensed by the state for the sale of Marijuana shops in their places.
“It’s all growing so fast that I don’t even know what to look forward to next,” says Daniel J. Oates, the police chief in Aurora in the east of Denver.
Representatives of the controlling bodies in the state of Washington also pacified scratch their heads.
They will have to formulate rules to determine the procedure for licensing the cultivation, processing, distribution and sale of marijuana, all this needs to be done before December 1, 2013.
They say that Colorado, it’s good or bad, outperanging the majority of other states in resolving issues of using Marijuana, first for medical purposes and now for amusement.
“Colorado has a more streamlined market, so they will serve as a good example,” explains Brian E. Smith, a spokeswoman for the Alcohol Purpose Regulatory and Selling of Alcoholic Pursuit.
But now no one is able to do more, Mr. Smith recognizes than put forward any propositions and hope that it will work.
“We don’t have a real precedent that we could focus on,” he said.
The Washington state bill, called I-502, will come into effect on December 6 and plunge the state into a state of uncertificate for an entire year, when state licensing systems do not yet exist, and the ownership of marijuana has already been legitimized.
And after all, there are still difficult technical aspects that need to be solved during this period, for example, how to find a balance between the state's order of “adequate access” to licensed marijuana with a ban on trading it at a distance of 1000 feet from schools, parks, playgrounds and kindergartens.
“Especially it will be difficult to place licensed marijuana-trafficking businesses, particularly in Seattle and its suburns,” says Ben Livingston, a spokeswoman for the newly formed Marijuana Legalization Research Group.
On November 21, Aurora’s police chief of the Aurora sent an e-mail message to his subordinates that the city’s office would no longer bring those who had reached the age of the age of the age of 21 to be responsible for owning a small amount of marijuana, and that the police stopped clinging to these irregularities “starting from now.”
The Oates police chief said that the police will monitor compliance in the city with rules governing the activities of persons raising marijuana for medical purposes and will prosecute illegitimate traffickers and drug distributors.
Weld County prosecutor in northern Colorado, Ken Buck, presents a stricter approach to this issue.
After the vote on the bill, he said that the prosecutor's office will continue to be prosecuted for possession of marijuana, mainly in order to indulge consecrucitant persons to undergo a course of treatment.
At the moment, 119 persons have been charged with storing two or less ounces of marijuana, although many of them are accused of other violations.
“The responsibility of our institution is to persecute actions that are deemed to be criminal at the time of their commission,” Buck said in a statement in a statement.
The reaction was ambigulate even in places such as Mesa’s Rural Cutinacy, where voters rejected a proposal to legalize marijuana.
Gran Ghankshn, the busiest city in the county, is no longer prosecuted by adults for owning a small amount of the drug.
Diocesan prosecutor Pete Hautzinger supported this decision, but also decided not to withdraw charges on already opened cases related to the possession of marijuana.
“I believe that to ensure compliance with the law for as long as it does not change is our direct responsibility,” he says.
Although 55% of Colorado residents who took part in the vote supported this decision, it will not be easy to include issues of using marjuana for amusement in the state and the legal system will not be easy.
And the contradictory reaction of residents in different parts of the state revealed the ambiguity of the attitude of the local authorities to the experiment of the state with marijuana.
“Digerant prosecutors in this matter have a cultural barrier in this matter,” Explains Sean McAllister, a Denver lawyer representing convicts for storing marjuana, and a local representative of the National Marijuana Legal Enforcement Organization.
“They persecuted the people for so long for this, that now they can’t really reconcile the fact that this is no longer a crime,” he says.
Colorado and D.N. - the first states in which a small amount of marjuana are treated in the same way as alcohol - is destined to become an experimental pearl on a national scale to test the very idea of legalizing drugs.
Proponents of legalization and state officials plan to expand the territory of legalized commerce, but at the same time they are awaiting instructions from the federal authorities, who still plan to consider the sale and cultivation of marijuana as a federal crime.
Proponents of the legalization of marijuana hope that the Justice Department will condone in this matter.
Despite a number of high-profficient arrests of merchants and patients who used marijuana for medical indications, the federal government in most cases gave the go-ahead of marijuana for medical purposes in Colorado, D.Dove and 16 other states.
Aegis of the Anti-Drutheric Anti-Drug Bureau are unlikely to break into homes to confiscate a small bag of marijuana, but they are unlikely to turn a blind eye to recreation marjuana stores regulated by the state authorities, which are allowed under a new law, says Kevin A. Sabeth, a former anti-drug advisor in the Obama administration.
Some cities in Colorado did not await the actions of the federal authorities.
Even even before the presidential election, some local authorities approved a moratorium on the opening of new marijuana stores, which could not open earlier than in a year.
Montourosis last week, a city in the western part of the state put forward a six-month ban on the sale of Marijuana, which is likely to be approved next week.
“We don’t want to end up in this position that we’ll issue a license to someone, and then it will cause serious problems at the federal level,” says Bob Nicholson, a member of the city council.
Our city voted against this amendment.
And now there is a contradiction between the results of the local vote and what the state voted for.
There are still a lot of unresolved issues.
Petronela Wyatt: I’ve survived from Oxford for being a Conservative.
Persecution of university students for their views did not begin today.
As far as I can remember, I’ve always dreamed of going to Oxford University.
My father and older similar studied at Oxford and described it as the best educational institution in the world, a modern analogue of an ancient symposium, laying the two foundations of civilization - free thought and tolerance.
And nevertheless, after two weeks of studying at the Faculty of History of Wooster's College at Oxford University in the late eighties, I collected my suitcases and left, which resulted in the first real scandal in my life.
My father couldn't stand it and cried.
The friends were confusing.
Newspaper The Evening Standard claimed that I dropped out of college because I didn't like that my classmates had sex in the next room.
Writer E. Nordmann Wilson joked that I was leaving, because I had to drink from squeezed cups.
It wasn't really so funny.
I've escaped.
Yes, she escaped, because I was systematically poisoned and intimidated.
It’s not because of my rather unusual name or the fact that I studied in a private high school.
I was persecuted only for one reason, and in this glorinious cradle of enlightenment it looked like real barbarism and blind fanaticism: my father, the now-numother of Woodrow Wyatt, was a well-known advisor to Margaret Totcher, and I myself was a supporter of Conservatives.
You will ask me, why do you remember this now?
According to the most recent data, a new generation of students, who adheres to center-right views, is subjected to the same bullying.
This institutionalized and turbulent self-concurrency of Oxford-Conservative students is so strong that last week a group of such students demanded the same protection of their rights as those provided to persons with persons with dissatisfaction, sexual and ethnic minorities.
Conservatives from Corpus Krysti College Student Club in Cambridge say that they “often are actively isolated, subjected to a personal attack, and make you feel unwanted guests” due to their political views.
They want to introduce an additional representative to the committee on the equal rights of the college in order to ensure the freedom to express their opinions.
Their situation only exacerbated the recently released BBC 2 documentary about politics in the student environment “Wondiversity Country: Young, Intelligent and Right”, in which Conservatives were presented with eccentrics and neo-Nazis.
In the film was shown by Joe Cake, the former president of the Oxford Conservative Association, who drives into a roll-royse, wears a silver suit and a canopy with a silver smadushnik.
In other Universities, Conservative students are complaining that they make “scapegoats due to increased tuition fees”.
Luke Blake, 20, vice-t-President of the Conservative Association of the University of Nottingham, in an interview with one of Sunday's newspapers said that "in a university's cross is increasing in the direction of leftist views.
People who don't even know us in advance consider us arrogant and unleased children of rich parents."
Samuel Roberts, for 21 years of age, a student of the Faculty of the History of the Corpus Kristi, who was petitioned for increased protection, calls such an atmosphere “uncomparable”, and Stefany Ceyll, 19, the new president of the Association of Conservatives of the University of Oxford, says the attitude of the members of the student’s club to the carriers of right-wing centrist views has deteriorated significantly.
“This poses a jeopardial to the atmosphere of an intellectual debate and simply for the normal life of club members,” she said.
For the first few weeks at Oxford, I wasn’t just a minority, I was alone.
I went there in September 1986, timid and shy eighteen-Year-Year-Year-Lithuanary.
“Oxford has freaked out of hatred for the Conservative Party.
A year earlier, the U.S. revoked former student Margaret Thatcher of her honorary degree due to a decrease in budget allocations for higher education.
In such an atmosphere it would be uncomfortuated even for Stalinists.
In the first days of the fresh grower, when students get acquainted with each other and with teachers, for the first time I felt what awaited me in front.
I did not yet know that educators not only looked through their fingers at bullying Conservative students, but were also happy to take part in it.
Politicular issues - the strike of miners, privatisation and disagreement of the government to apply sanctions against the apartheid government in South Ark - infiltrated the walls of auditoriums covered with wooden panels.
At my first seminar, I had to translate the eighteenth-eater French text into an eighteenth century, and I wasn’t prepared for what followed.
“Miss Wyatt,” the lecturer, now the late Harry Pitt said, “please translate the first paragraph.”
It’s been difficult for me, I’m stuck.
Harry Pitt was very bile man with a Cake-skinned face.
“Thatcherists don’t really teach the French or do they just lack the brains?” he said.
Other students gravitated.
In my eyes there were tears in front of my eyes.
