The most difficult profession in the world: miners from Kawah-Ijen
For the four-euro, the Indonesian miners are at risk of life and health, dragging 70 kilograms of sulfur at the top of a volcano along steep stony trails
There are people for whom their work - hell, and others literally work in hell.
Among them, Anto Vijaya, one of the 400 miners who earn a sulfur pregnit on life in Kawah-Jigen Volbulance, located in the east of the Indonesian island of Java.
Every day Anto descends to the very bottom of the crater, where sulfur gas rising from the depths of the Earth, freezes when in contact with the air.
Coupling huge chubles of sulfur, the total weight of which can reach 70 kg, he puts them in two bamboo baskets, puts carts on their shoulders and carries them up on a steep stony road.
To the top of the volcano, whose height is 2.386 meters, only 250 meters, but it takes more than 40-minute grippers to climb a cruciferal step, with difficulty keeping the equilibrium and gently grafting the road so as not to stumble and not to fall from the cliff.
They know that one incorrect step may cost them their lives, as it happened with one tourist from France, who broke down a few years ago from the cliff of Cavakh-Ijena.
Chemistres from Kawah-Ijena earn 5 euro cents for every kilogram of sulfur, which is mined
Ending upstairs, they pave the way between the tourists, who are photographed, as if they were monkey from a circus and, it’s getting tired of carrying heavy cartridges, walking three kilometres to the scales that the mining company placed a little bit higher, at an altitude of 1,850 meters.
We are talking about TT Kandi Ngrimbi, an enterprise that has been exploiting this volcano since 1960 and, it's better and not to say, its Employees, which it pays 662 Indonesian ropes (5 euro cents) per kilo of sulfur per kilo of sulfur.
Then it sells for 10,000 rupees (83 euro cents) in petrochemical industries, because this mineral is common in everyday life and is used to make matches, fireworks, cosmetics, dynamite and even for bleaching of sugar.
“Since the norm is carrying 70 kilograms, we earn about 46,000 rupees (3.8 euros) for each walking race,” Anto, who usually performs three daily carriers.
He spends three hours for each of them and at the end feels broken, but they allow him to accrue 138.000 rupees (11,5 euros).
Although this seems like a miser for such an inhumane effort, it’s three times more that he would make money in the field.
“Mayboard for miners is very high according to local standards, where 15,000 rupees (1.2 rupees) per day and the average monthly earnings are equal to two million rupees (167 euros),” explains the mottman who previously worked as a masculator on the tourist island of Bali.
There his salary was 75.000 rupees (6.2 euros a day and the plot was not so heavy, but Anto returned with his family to Banyuwangi, a town not far from the volcano, for a good reason, which in Indonesia is as devastating as gray: "I married a girl from Bali, where only dynasies, and brought her to Java so that she accepted Islam."
Anto asthma, he breathes with difficulty, constantly coughs, and his eyes are watered because of toxic gases
At the age of 27, Anto spent for three years, risking herself in Kawah-Ijen, whose sero has already began to affect him despite the presence of a mask and special glasses.
His asthma, he breathes with difficulty, constantly coughs, and his eyes get pounded due to the toxic gases that the volcano emits.
This is the price that he has to pay to achieve his dream.
“I will work for another two years, because I want to open a store or study Spanish or a French one,” he promises in a more than acceptable English.
Pusted by life, this pleasant and intelligent young man could be a tourist guide, a waiter or porter in a hotel, but instead he performs mule work.
Deliberate a dirty wooden hut with other porticians, it rises every day at two o'clock in the morning, because sulfur doesn't stop flowing at night, when its characteristic yellow color turns into a blue and shines among the darkness.
Challenging the dungeon, Anto descends into a crater, illuminating the way for a small lantern connected to his helmet, which he himself bought with his own money.
Approximately 400 porticians are dragging groceries with squeeze on their shoulders from the Bottom of a Volcan
Despite high yields, the mining company did not mechanize sulfur production to save costs and does not provide any equipment to porters who work in their own account and with such deprivations.
In fact, they don’t even get part of 30,000 rupees (2.5 euros) collection for photography, which, along with an entrance ticket for 15,000 rupees (1.2 euros), the guards of this reserve charge tourists who come to take a picture of the volcano and its mule people.
“It’s a job for livestock, not for people,” says Badrusin, a 42-year-old-mountained portician who spent three decades in Kawah-Ijen since he left the college.
Capable of raising up to 110 kilograms, he assures that he will endure the work “as long as he can”, because he needs money to educate his three children between the ages of 18 and 10.
I won't re-recrebrium, I'm going to die here, because the volcano is my whole life
Although sulfur strifts the throat and burns the eyes when the wind suddenly changes and catches miners in dense pillars of smoke coming out of the volcano, they are so resilient that no one complains about its serious diseases ... more serious than their usual breathing problems, arthrosis, knee and ulcer on the shoulders, which have deformed under the weight of the carts.
Balancing the cart on the back, Unaynik now can only lift 50 kilograms in his 53 years.
Every day he and his comrades take 15 tons of sulphur from the volcano, which three trucks are transported to a warehouse in Tamansari, overcoming a distance of 18 kilometers along a narrow path among the thickets.
“I won’t re-released, I’m going to die here, because the volcano is my whole life,” Unaynik said, revealing his mouth that doesn’t miss a few teeth.
Of its five children, the eldest, 30 years old also works as a sulfur transmittone.
Time passes, but poverty perpetuates from generation to generation one of the heaviest activities in the world: what human mules from Kawah-Idzhen are doing.
