The UNASR summit ends without procluding the Lima Declaration
The VI Presidential Summit of the Union of South American Nations (UNASUR) ended today in Peru without proclamation of the Lima Declaration, announced earlier and theoretically signed by seven mandatory participants.
Efe has repeatedly attempted to gain access to a document signed at a regular meeting of heads of state and government of UNASUR, but sources from the Office and the Presidential Administration indicated on the main page that it would be handed over after cloumination, but then confirmed that at a certain point it would be published on the website of the Peruvian Government.
To the questions about the text, they pointed out that the content was stated by the President of Peru, Olyant Umala, during brief statements for the press.
Journalists' access to information about the Summit has always been restricted.
During the day it was only broadcasted, in the press hall, a video of the presidential assembly without a sound with the message “closed meeting, sound is prohibited”.
Slight information circulating among the reporters was provided by the spokespersons of some UNASUR governments present at the meeting, but not Peruvian.
The only document distributed during the day was a list of participating presidents, which caused dissatisfaction of hundreds of journalists from various national and international media, who asked for more information, were dissatisfied.
Subsequent subsequently, the administration of the President of Peru sent an electronic message to the media with the “final declaration” of the summit, but we are talking about Umaly’s statement, and not about the official document that ended the summit.
Last October, Peru hosted the Third South American and Arab (ASPA Summit), and at that time, despite the fact that it was persistently demanded by the press, also the so-called Lima Declaration, which had previously been pronounced.
On the official ASP website, it can be verified that the document was published last Tuesday.
At both international meetings, the Peruvian authorities tried to ensure transmission systems for all journalists were ensured, but as far as possible to obtain information.
The summit also culminated in a joint decision of Chile-Purpose to pass the verdict of the Hague Court, which will settle the territorial dispute between the two countries.
The presidents of Peru, Olyanta Umaala, and Chile, Sebastian Piñera, gathered at a regional meeting and have ratified that they would abide by the International Court of Judgment, which begins hearing statements by both sides on Monday, as part of an accomplice Lima put forward against Santiago.
“We will comply and execute the decision that will clarify the differences that we are currently presenting to this international court,” Umaala said, next to his Chilean counterpart.
“Chili was, is and will be a country that respects international law, a peaceful resolution of disagreements, pacts and international tribunals,” Pinier added, and greeted Umalu squeeches against the back of the flags of these two countries.
Confession by the two presidents that they will obey the International Court took place immediately after Colombia had challenged the Bogota Pahé this week, according to which it recognized subordination to the verdicts of this international court following the decision on maritime borders from Nicaragua, which it considered erroneous.
The summit took place in the absence of Brazilian presidents, Dilma Rousseff; Venezuela, Hugo Chávez; Bolivia, Evo Morales; and Argentina, Cristina Kirchner.
Paragwy, whose membership in UNASUR has been suspended since 2011 after the impeachment of its former President Fernando Lugo, did not participate in the meeting.
Accepting president, Ollent Umaala, was responsible for opening discussions in the in the morning and shaping the summit, a little after noon in Lima’s time.
The head of state read the final document, in which it said that 16 agreements were adopted and actions on 31 projects were approved among South American countries, the amount of estimated investments amounted to $ 17 billion.
Among the adopted agreements, he mentioned that UNASUR countries will take "important steps towards the goal of South American citizenship, for which we are extending residency agreements."
He said that actions are being taken to improve "cooperation in the fight against citizens' insecurities and against organized transnational crime, an action to increase the availability of medicines, affordable Internet access in all corners of South America and to take into account the cumulative and effective risks of natural disasters."
Since Europe is in a crisis, “economic ununion (Latin America) should not have a trivinatory framework, but should serve to expand our production foundation and anticipate a better future for our cities,” Umala added.
“We decided to give privileges to a group of 31 iconic projects that will improve the connection of South American spaces, especially in timber and border zones ... by uniting our countries and forming new economic schemes”, – said the Peruvian president in a reading message.
Among these projects, he mentioned that the five belonged to Peru and are confined to cross axes on its territory, from the coast to Brazil, and the two are aiming for a greater connection with Ecuador, but it did not provide any more details.
Also, the final document touched the political situation in Paraguay.
“We hope that the electoral process in this country will enjoin its rejoicing in the Union of South American nations”, from which it is currently excluded.
The need for Latin America to remain a region of well-benefficial and peace would be united, and have good relations among its neighbords to be another topic emphasized at the summit.
