“Southern U.A. should learn from Europe on the integration of citizenship”: Raphael Correa
The President of Ecuador, Raphael Correa, confirmed today that the creation of a common citizenship is the goal in which “the South America, in this case, yes, must learn from Europe.”
Korrea, who participated in the 6th Presidential Summit of the Union of South American NATIONS (UNASUR), held in Lima, told the state-of-the-Laith of Peru that Europeans “killed each other during the Second world war” and other clashes, “but now they are almost one father-in-law.”
He defended, in this context, a project to establish South American citizenship, which is approved by the countries participating in IN ASUR.
“It is necessary to achieve the free movement of citizens and workers in any South American country, as it already happens with the members of the Andean community, but there are still reactionary sectors that want to return us to the past,” he said.
The Ecuadorian President has also shown himself a supporter of the resumption of the Organization of American States (OAG) under the presuppose of reducing the influence of Anglo-Saxon states and taking into account those who signed the San Jose Human Rights Pact.
Those who give a lecture, never take on any obligations, and we, South Americans, on the other hand, on the contrary, sign everything.
“Unexplained that the Inter-Amerian Human Rights Commission is located in D.C. and financed by the United States,” he said, hinting at a political asylum granted to WikiLeaks founder Giulian Assange.
Korrea assured that he does not regret this decision, because he did not betray his principles with him, but observed his “deep values of democracy and human rights”.
He added that at one time “there were certain suspicions that Assange will be extradited to a third country and will not observe the process.”
In addition, he criticized the Swedish justice for demanding to arrive at its territory for interrogation of an alleged sexual crime, while “the Swedish law itself considers it possible to carry it through a videoconferenvironment that could be drawn from the Embassy of Ecuador in London”.
Korrea confirmed that there is a risk of a deteriorating physical and mental health of Assange.
“I haven’t talked to him since he arrived at our embassy, but the ambassador informed me that he had a small pulmonary problem, nothing serious,” the Ecuadorian head of state said.
What really there is is the danger of deteriorating his physical and mental health due to imprisonment in a small space without the opportunity to do exercises on the air.
“It affects the health of any person,” he added.
Korrea pointed out that the solution to the asylum issue provided to Assange since June at the Ecuadorian Embassy in London, by providing a guarantee of inviolability that will allow him to go to Ecuador is in the hands of Great Britain, Sweden and European judicial authorities and stressed that there had been negotiations in London to find a solution to the fact that WikiLeaks found a foundger.
We don’t negotiate human rights, we don’t use that word in this case, but there have been constant discussions.
“Resolution of this problem lies in the hands of the United Kingdom, Sweden and the European judicial authorities because Assange’s lawyer, Baltasar Garzon, conducts a number of processes in various European instances,” he said.
“If the U.K. provides a guarantee of inviolability tomorrow, it will end.”
And if Sweden, as its legislation perfectly allows it and how it has done in other occasions, will question Señor Assange at the Embassy of Ecuador in London or will question him on Skype, tomorrow this problem will have been exhausted.
Korrea took the opportunity to express himself as a defender of freedom of the press and stressed that he does not tolerate "mediocrity, unscrupulousness and lies that distort freedom of expression."
“The main enemies of freedom of the press are not spoiled villains-politics, but bad journalists who support profit, blackmail and extortion”, – he said. – he said.
In this aspect, he remained pleased that neither these journalists, “neither bankers nor bourgeois hegeamon countries do not prevail in Ecuador”, and added that, being re-elected, it “will deepen the revolution to continue development in the right direction.”
Korrea also backed the decision to keep the Paraguayan veto in UNASUR at least until next elections, arguing that the organization “should be steadfaytious and not tolerate opportunism and the support of coups under the masculine of legality,” because in reality “this has shattered the legality of the Paraguayan demise.”
The Ecuadorian President also considered, in addition, the desire of his Colombian counterpart Juan Manuel Santos to now negotiate maritime borders between the two countries after the decision of the International Court of Justice of The Hague, which turned out in favor of Nicaraguan maritime sovereignty.
At the moment, this decision has not been complied with.
This is a problem between the South American and Central American countries.
Conflicts are unavoidable, but they must be overcame the desire to go together.
It is Necessary Necessarily Treat them together in order to overcome them and keep moving forward.
He also expressed the hope for a good end to the dispute about the maritime borders, which is conducted in the same court of Peru and Chile, and said that “it is correct for Latin America to apply to international bodies, if the two countries undertake to make a decision, no matter how difficult it may be”.
Concerning the opportunity to act as a candidate in the upcoming presidential elections in Ecuador in order to run for a period of time in a row, he pointed out that he is considering this possibility “with great optimism and joy, although sometimes it is quite difficult”.
Correa assured that if he loses the elections in February 2013, she will go away from public life.
In my personal speculation, I was never interested in power, but in such unjust situations as in Ecuador, social and economic poverty can only be corrected by political power.
“My political movement considered that it was I who guaranteed this likely victory, so we have to recognize this responsibility,” he said.
In case of victory, this would become my last term as president, and then we will leave public life.
If we're going to lose, it's the same.
“This is a decision,” he confirmed.
Correa also mentioned a new course of treatment for Venezuelan President Hugo Chávez in Cuba.
“I’ve just spoken to Venezuelan Vice President Nicolas Maduro, who told me he’d gone for a treatment that had already been planned, routine, and it’s expected to miss a campaign to go back to Cuba.
“This doesn’t mean exacerbating President Chávez’s health,” he said.
