The Mortgage of AIDS Today - the Result of Late Diagnosance
Fabrizio was at the age of 21, when it was announced that his AIDS analysis had yielded a positive result.
“It’s kind of jolted me down,” he recalls that difficult, despite the whole doctor’s betting.
The young man hid this from his family.
Deciding to cope with the disease on his own, he wanted to learn as much as possible about it. And his efforts were justified - he has already celebrated his 43 th birthday.
Fabrizio - one of the "eldimpledest" patients of the Hospital Civil de Guadalajara (HCG), where he got in 1994, overcame a lot of hurtens.
He lives with a diagnosis of human immunodeficiencies virus (HIV) for 22 years. In the beginning of the 90-ies it would have been difficult to believe - there were too few treatment options at the time, too much knew about the disease, too much related to the patients.
So, even the director of the clinic of the Mexican social protection Institute did not want to say goodbye to it, "because he had a cut."
In other words, in those days to have an AIDS was a verififul.
Now it is possible to survive in this syndrome and do it without impairing the quality of life.
Nevertheless, without being aware of their illness, so far many people come when the virus has already caused the detriment, “descended” their immune system and they suffer from opportunistic infections.
31 years after the emergence of an AIDS in the world, at least in the aftermath of what has already been stated, “a great achievement at the moment is the fact that the survival of a patient who begins treatment in a timely manner and the survival of the general population is exactly the same,” said the head of the HCG HIV Department, Jaime Andrade Viljanueva, citing the fact that this information was confirmed in April this year in a prestigious scientific publication.
Infectionist and expert on HIV/Aid, Andrade Villeanueva explained that since 2008, scientists came to the conclusion that AIDS is not a verdict, but the duration and quality of life depends on the degree of lesion of the immune system available in patients at the beginning of the diagnosis, with better life expectancy for those who do not use drugs: up to 30 years with the number of CD4, and up to the age of 50 when it is reported.
Simultaneously speaking, the fore said means that someone who receives an HIV-positive diagnosis at the age of 25, according to these terms, “by maintaining control, will be able to live without problems up to the age of 75,” he explained.
To assess this progress, it should be remembered that the life expectancy of Mexicans today is an averaging 76 years.
Although mortality has declined significantly in recent years in the case of Mexico, the number of people dying of AIDS has fallen from 6,678 in 2007 to 4.862 in 2011 (YUNAIDS ' an annual report), it's also true that 60 per cent of patients in a national database have died since the adverbrevilescence of AIDS.
In Jalisco alone, 255 people have died in 2011, and 187 deaths have already been recorded by May this year; nevertheless, it is claimed that there is widespread access to anti-retroviral drugs since 2005.
- Why do you still have deaths?
It seems to me personally that the problem is not access to treatment.
That's what I'll see, it's what's going on in our hospital.
At least for the last 12 years we did not have a shortage of medicines, the problem is that patients come very late, because we do not know about their infected state, that is, in the late stages of the disease.
"Nine out of ten patients come when they already have any opportunist infection; what needs to be done to have a greater impact on the overall mortality rate is to diagnose at an earlier stage, and therefore it's necessary to provide samples to detect on a massive scale, for all the people who will be asked."
In their proposal, experts and officials of the State AIDS Prevention Council in Chalisco (COESIDA) converge, as well as patients like Fabrizio themselves, who turned to a private laboratory, motivated only by the fact that his a friend and, despite his young age, he was already at the stage of an AIDS and even suffered from a sarcoma Kaposha, a cancerous tumor.
Everything changes when you know you have an AIDS.
Some people think they’re going to die and they don’t want to know.
"If I'm going to die anyway, I'd rather try three times a week," they say, but not in my case.
There are positive changes, I eat well, do exercises, I take medication.
To this day, his parents only know that he has had the cancer.
I live a normal life, like any other.
“I am working, I have a lot of classes, I am traveling, having an active sexual life, but with mutual responsibility, I take care of myself and for the other person,” Fabrizio said, who agreed to share his personal life with MILENIO JALISCO in order to motivate those who, in the Context of the World AIDS Day, are afraid.
Let them check if there was a risk of infection, let them know as soon as possible whether they are HIV-positive, so better, and if they already have a diagnosis, let them find out that they can live like any other person, being responsible.
This message summarizes the fight against AIDS in this year of 2012.
Confections behind the shelf
The gulpieces between health programs and a simple citizen, says Ricardo Salazar, a Guadalajara journalist who was dealing with a problem related to HIV.
And the biggest is prevention.
In the places devoted to this task, "in fact the spread of condoms has increased, before we were given one or two, now they give us packages a hundred, and this is very good, but it turns out that there are those who now do not yet have access to the condom," he said.
Adolescents among the most vulnerable to new infections are teenagers.
"Why are they for you?" this is a popular question, with sneezing and condemnation, on the part of social workers, school consultants, employees of pharmacies and health care workers who teenagers do not want to hear, the propagandist assured the propagandist.
Suggest to change this inefficienious distribution; so that condoevities are not only behind the shelfs and that a Hundundred of them is wearing vintages in public toilets in places that are often visited by young people.
It’s not an enthusiment of promiscues.
We are not talking about paying beer and a motel, Governor Emilio Gonzalez replied when he was questioned whether it is possible to distribute consolidates under his leadership.
“This is not sexuality, it is best to make consocietals available to those who already practice sexual activity,” he said.
Key data in Jalisco
There were 13.435 total cases (12,158 and 1,317 HIV).
The state ranks number 4 in the national ranking for new and cumulative cases of AIDS and the 13th for HIV.
92% of incinerators relate to the genital tract, 6% through the blood, and 2 perinatial.
