That team since the 1990 FIFA World Cup is the best team you've ever seen?
It is among the best, as well as the one that she has benefited from the Dream Team at the Olympic Games of the year of 2004.
But my favorite was the one from the Pan-American Games in Cuba in 1991, when we scored gold and smash the U.S. national team, which was quite similar to the one who benefited from our bronze at the World Cup.
In that team not only again played Mintsi, Gauss, Ramona Rivas, Phiko López and “Piculina” (Ortiz), but also young (Javier) “Tonyito” Colón and James Carter, brothers León (Francisco and Edgar) and Mario “Kihote” Morales, who could not participate in the year 90 due to a Knee injury.
The team, which may not be the best in terms of personnel, but brought us a gold medal and a lot of joy, was a team of the 1995 Olympic qualifying tournament in Neuquene, Argentina.
With role-playing players such as Canito Nevés, Pablo Alicea and the young Rolando Khurruitner, instead of players temporarily suspended for a mess at the Mar del Plata Pan-American Championship, we won the gold in defied of all forecasts.
Who was the best player in Puerto Rico?
Undoubtedly, it was Pikulin Ortiz.
His indicators at international tournaments are impressive.
No one in Puerto Rico dominated at this level as Pikulin did.
And it doesn’t take into account his quarrel in the various leagues in which he played.
Who was the best Puerto Rican leader?
It's a very difficult question.
We had a very good young, including Julio Thoro, Flora Mélènez, Carlos Morales, Raymond Dalmau, Armando Torres.
Of the young, I really like the work of Leo Arill.
What do you think is your main achievement in the federation?
Participated in the most glorious era of the national national team between 1988 and 1995, and that in the early 90-ies BSN there were up to 17 teams per season.
What do you have to do?
There were things that I wanted to implement, for example, the regionalization of smaller categories.
For example, that boys from Ponse play only in their field and only against teams from other parts of the island in the national playoffs.
Now the guys are traveling and playing too much, and this is superfluous.
At the very least, I can see the benefits of mandatory certification and a course for managers, officials and judges.
This gives me a pleasure.
What are you doing right now?
Most of all I listen to the music, I watch the musical videos of my era on YouTube, enjoy my grandchildren and occasionally go to basketball matches.
And, of course, I am enjoying the company of my wife, Apple, who has always been with me.
Died actor Larry Hagman
Larry Hagman, who was born on September 21, 1931 in Fort-Werte (Texas), became a world-renowned for his main role as John Ross Ewing, better known as "J.R.", in the Dallas television series in which he embodyed a businesswoman without principles, malicious and manipulative.
Larry Hagman, whose the role of the oil grinerten de proprietary J.R. Ewing in the Dallas TV series became a symbol of greed in the 1980-ies, has died.
He was 81 years old.
Hagman, who came back this year as J.R. in the new season of “Dallas”, died on Friday night due to complications caused by cancer, according to a family statement provided to The Associated Press by Warner Bros.
Larrie again returned to his beloved hometown of Dallas, again playing an iconic role that he liked the most,” his family said.
Larry’s family and the closest friends were with him in Dallas at a Thanksgiving fair.
Linda Gray, who played the role of his wife in the original series and in the sequel, was close next to Hagman when he died at Dallas hospital, said its representative Jeffrey Lane.
He brought joy to all those who knew him.
He was creative, generous, cheerful, affectionate and talented, and I will miss him very much.
“He was an original personality and lived a full life,” Gray said in a statement.
Hagman was diagnosed with cirrhosis of the cirrhosis of the liver in 1992 and he admitted that he drank for many years.
In the year in 1995 he found a malcomfort tumor in the liver and he underwent a transplant.
Long before Dallas, Hagman gained notoriety on television thanks to his role as a decent-seater comedy "I Dream of Jeannie", which aired NBC from 1965 to 1970.
In it, he played Captain Tony Nelson, an astronaut whose life changes when he meets an attractive genie girl playing Barbara Eden and begins to live with her under the same roof.
He was also the main character of two comedies, which rarely appeared on the air, "The Good Life" (NBC, 1971-72) and "Here We Go Again" (ABC, 1973).
His work in the cinema included roles well-accustomed by critics in "The Group", "Harry and Tonto" and "Pprimary Colors".
But it was his mastermind interpretation the delightfully disgusting J.R. brought Hagman at the top of his fame.
Dramantic CBS series about Ewing clan and other Characters was aired between April 1978 and May 1991.
The promotional prophet, “Who Shouted at J.R.?”, conceived to create hype around an episode full-of-embezzlement in which Hagman’s character is almost killed, gave rise to international discussions and the millions of dollars they risked in betting offices.
Also, it also helped to attract a record audience at that time to the series.
When the answer was given in an episode from November 1980, an average of 41 million viewers watched it and turned Dallas into the second number of views of the entertainment program in the story, after the "MASH" final in 1983, attracted 50 million spectators.
It was a sister-in-chieter of J.R., Kristin (performed by Mary Crosby), who shot him.
J.R. interfered her and then ignored her that she would accuse her of prostitution if she did not get out of the city, but the others also had a reason to attack him.
Hagman played Ewing as an unsatisfactory corrupist with a charismatic smile: a dishonest entrepreneur and unfaithful husband who wanted his wife an alcoholic, Sue Ellen (Lynda Gray), placed for treatment.
“I’m known what it should be on the graving stool of J.R,” Hagman said in 1988.
It must be said: "Here lies an honest citizen J.R. Ewing."
It's the only deal he's lost.
Quinia Prinsipal, also the heroine of the original TV series, recalled on Friday Hagman as someone “huge, both on and off the screen”.
It is unforgettable and irrelevant for millions of admirers around the world and in the hearts of every one of us who was lucky enough to know and to love him.
Ten episodes of Dallas was shown a few months ago with a great success on the TNT channel.
Already completed the recording of five episodes of the second of the second season and the sixth was in production, according to the channel.
Warner or TNT haven’t yet made statements about what’s going to happen to Hagman since Hagman leaves.
A native of Fort-Werta, Texas, he was the son of the actress and singer Mary Martin, who shone in such classical works as "South Pacific" and "Peter Pan".
Martin was still a teenager when she gave birth in 1931 during her marriage to lawyer Ben Hegman.
He tried his happies on the theatrical stages of New York in the early 1950’s, and then served in the British Air Force from 1952 to 1956.
